#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""attavo: the command.

    attavo doctor [TENANT_ROOT] [--json]
    attavo spec-gate [N]
    attavo approve [N] --by LOGIN
    attavo approval-status [N]
    attavo build [N]
    attavo verify [N]
    attavo file-findings [N]
    attavo file-stops [N]
    attavo counsel [N]
    attavo record-only
    attavo attest [--partial] [N]
    attavo drive [N] [--until-ready]
    attavo ledger [N] [--json]
    attavo stops [N] [--json]

The doctor is the definition of done. It asserts five proofs against a
tenant root and exits 1 until every proof holds:

  1. every-gate-rejects      every gate in the manifest's [gates] table,
                             run against its rejection fixture, exits non-zero,
                             and carries a non-empty retires
  2. one-item-every-stage    one work item under paths.records carries a
                             record for every stage -- spec, approval,
                             build, verify -- all four introduced by one
                             merge commit reachable from HEAD
  3. no-commit-lacks-record  every commit on the default branch after the
                             manifest was introduced is named by a record's
                             sha or commits list, or itself writes under
                             paths.records (a record is committed with the
                             code it describes; an item's merge commit
                             carries its own record paths this way)
  4. no-stop-lacks-cause     every build or verify record under
                             paths.records at record_version 3 or later,
                             with a status other than pass, carries a stop
                             naming an enumerated class and provenance, a
                             non-empty detail when unclassed, the gates
                             heading when a build failed after running
                             them, and the tool's own error line when an
                             adapter exit ended the log -- records before
                             record_version 3 are counted and reported,
                             never refused
  5. every-attested-item-is-sealed
                             every merged item that carries a record for
                             every stage has a statement in the forge's
                             attestation store for its merge commit, read
                             through [forge].attestation_command -- a
                             merge at or before [forge].sealed_since is
                             named as before the window instead of failed

Nothing in doctor is a gate. The doctor reads; it never writes and it never
blocks a merge by itself. It is what CI runs to say whether the product
is done.

spec-gate, approve, and approval-status are gates and writers. They run
from the tenant root (the current working directory); N, the work item id,
is read from the ATTAVO_ITEM environment variable when set, else from the
branch (attavo/N-*). The doctor's first proof sets ATTAVO_ITEM for a
rejection fixture's own item, so a gate's own command needs no argument
even while the enclosing repository sits on a real item's attavo/N-*
branch. Every run of spec-gate or approve writes the next numbered attempt
file under paths.records/N/ and never overwrites one already written. See
the spec contract in the project's Phase 1 issue for the full rules each
one enforces.

build refuses (exit 1, naming the cause, no record) unless the manifest
declares [executor].build and [tests].select and [tests].run, and unless
approval-status would print valid. It exports the executor's identity
(issue #33) into the adapter's environment as GIT_AUTHOR_NAME/EMAIL and
GIT_COMMITTER_NAME/EMAIL, set by the product and never the tenant's git
config, then invokes the named adapter (adapters/<name>/run) with the
spec, the corpus, and the branch. It refuses (status refused) a commit
between start_sha and HEAD not authored by that identity, checks that
every path touched matches a spec claim, records the adapter's own
declared "reports" -- the fields adapters/<name>/conformance.json says
it can supply with a real value (issue #41), so a field it claims and
never supplies is a defect the conformance suite catches rather than a
silent null -- then runs the criteria, the
tests [tests].select and [tests].run name for the touched paths, and --
when the manifest declares [checks].command -- the tenant's own
whole-tree check, from the tenant root. pass requires every criterion,
the test run, and the tenant's checks (if declared) to exit 0; any
non-zero exit iterates, with the failure as feedback, up to a fixed
budget. [checks] is optional; a tenant that declares none is not
refused. It writes the next build-NN.json under paths.records/N/, with
the adapter's transcript -- each iteration's gate output (criteria
exits, the selected tests' stdout, the tenant's checks' tail) appended
under an "--- attavo gates, iteration N ---" heading, issue #89 -- beside
it as build-NN.log. Every record whose status is not pass carries stop:
{class, provenance, detail}, derived from the refusal, halted, and log
already on hand (issue #89); a pass record carries stop: null. See the
build contract in the project's Phase 2 issue for the full rules it
enforces.

verify refuses (exit 1) unless the item's latest build record has
status pass, and unless the manifest declares [forge].checks_command --
a tenant command, taking a full commit sha on argv, that prints one
line per check as "<name> <conclusion>". verify runs it on the build
record's sha first: any conclusion other than success, neutral, or
skipped writes verify-NN.json with status returned and calls no
adapter, because a judge is never spent on a tree the forge already
called red. With the forge green, verify calls the adapter named by
[executor].verify (defaulting to [executor].build) with the diff since
the build's approval, the spec, and the build record, and records its
findings and its declared "reports" (issue #41). [executor.<adapter>.<stage>] shadows [executor.<adapter>] for
build or verify alone; both merge parent then stage before setting
ATTAVO_ADAPTER_CONFIG, and the record's adapter_config is the merged
result. Like build, every verify record whose status is not pass
carries stop: {class, provenance, detail} (issue #89); a pass record
carries stop: null. file-findings opens one tenant issue per finding in
the item's latest verify record via [forge].file_issue_command, and
refuses (exit 1, nothing filed) a record carrying a finding with no
retires. See the verify contract in the project's Phase 2 issue for the
full rules both enforce.

file-stops opens one tenant issue per stop record of the item with no
issue filed for it yet, through [forge].file_issue_command, naming the
class, the provenance, the record path, and the log's last twenty
lines; a record it has already filed carries a <stage>-NN.filed marker
beside it, holding the filing command's own stdout, so a second run
files nothing new. Like a build or verify record, this command writes
the marker and leaves it uncommitted -- drive commits it, in the same
commit as the record it names (issue #89). It refuses (exit 1,
nothing filed) a stop whose class is unclassed and whose detail is
empty -- a stop the product cannot even describe is not filed as if it
could. See issue #89's lane for the full rules it enforces.

counsel reads the item's latest non-pass build or verify record -- or,
when neither exists, its latest refused spec-gate record, the earliest
point a question can be answered inside the product, before any
approval or executor spend (issue #133) -- and asks the tenant's
[executor].counsel adapter (defaulting to [executor].verify) a
read-only question about it: the record, its log, the spec (problem,
claims, criteria, stub, including stub/.removed), paths.rulings, the
item's diff since approval, the tenant manifest, and this product's own
root (bin/attavo, adapters/, schema/, templates/) by path, under the
same sandbox verify runs under. The adapter answers with class (the
stop enumeration, or unclassed), verdict (answer: a ruling text with
citations; patch: a product defect's file, symbol, rejection test, and
diff; person: a one-line question no rule covers), citations
(instruction, mechanism, guard, each a path and symbol or a ruling id),
and proposal (the ruling text, the patch, or the question). It writes
the next counsel-NN.json under paths.records/N/, with the adapter's
transcript beside it as counsel-NN.log, and commits nothing -- the
caller commits, as with every stage. It refuses (exit 1, nothing
written) when the item carries no non-pass build, verify, or spec-gate
record to counsel. See issue #100's lane for the full rules it
enforces, and issue #133's for the spec-gate fallback.

record-only exits 0 when HEAD, against its first parent, touches only
paths.records; 1 otherwise; 2 with no manifest. Core logic about a git
checkout, not a forge's shell -- a workflow template's push-and-retrigger
guard is one line reading this exit code instead of carrying its own
copy of the check.

attest refuses (exit 1) unless the latest spec, approval, build, and
verify records for an item all exist and chain: the build's approval_sha
names the latest approval, and the verify's build_sha names that build --
a re-approval after a build must force a re-run of what changed. With no
N given it derives the item from the record paths HEAD's merge commit
touched under paths.records/<N>/, not from a branch name, which a squash
merge discards and which names a forge's convention the product does not
know; more than one item derived is a refusal naming what it found. Zero
is not a defect -- not every merge is an item merge -- exit 0, nothing
printed but a line saying so.

attest never writes. On a coherent chain it prints the attestation
statement -- validating against schema/attestation.schema.json -- to
stdout as JSON and exits 0: the item, its four records and their shas,
and the commit the statement was computed at (sha and merge_sha are the
same commit here; on the merge group that commit is the one that lands).
The four stage records already under paths.records are the evidence for
that statement; a forge-specific template signs the printed JSON,
unmodified, as the predicate, against the commit that lands, on the merge
group -- and the signed bundle in the forge's attestation store is the
seal over it. attest itself never signs anything and never talks to a
signing service. --partial (issue #67) is for a pull request, where the
record a complete chain would need is the output of a later stage that is
itself waiting on this check: a missing later record is "not yet" (exit
0, naming the item and the stages present and absent, no statement
printed) rather than a refusal, while the two chain assertions -- build's
approval_sha names the latest approval, verify's build_sha names the
latest build -- still run over whatever records exist. merge_group and a
push to main still demand the whole chain. See issue #69's lane for the
full rules it enforces.

drive reads an item's latest records and a forge's own check conclusions
and performs the next mechanical step, and only that step, then stops: no
valid approval refuses by name and runs nothing; no passing build bound to
that approval runs build; a passing build with the forge green on the
branch head (polled for a bound, not the manifest) runs verify; a passing
verify with the forge still green prints that the item is ready to merge
and stops there -- the merge is never drive's own act, and it enqueues
nothing. A record build or verify writes that is not pass is counseled (counsel,
issue #100) and filed (file-stops, issue #89) before it is transported,
so its .filed marker exists in time to be committed alongside it; drive
then commits the stage record and the counsel record together -- each
with its own log and marker -- with `git commit --only`, never sweeping
the index, and pushes; a push a divergent remote rejects
is retried once after `git pull --no-rebase --no-edit`, never a rebase,
since the record already names the commit's sha. drive writes no record
of its own: a second run in the same state repeats the same step or the
same stop, reading exactly what the prior run left behind -- the marker
included, so a stop already filed is never filed again, from this
worktree or a fresh one. See issue #84's lane for the full rules it
enforces.

--until-ready (issue #100's own followups) repeats step-and-wait --
step, the forge poll already inside a step, step again -- in the same
process, rather than making the caller invoke drive again after every
passing step, until the item is ready to merge or a step stops on its
own account (a refusal, a halted or failed or refused record, or the
forge never going green); a step that stops still prints exactly what
the same step would print without the flag. Without --until-ready,
drive is unchanged: one step, then stop, exactly as before.

ledger reads, never writes. For item N (or every item under
paths.records when N is omitted), it walks the commits from the item's
first spec commit to its merge (the same derivation doctor's proof two
and attest use) or to HEAD when the item has not merged, and prints one
row per act: the sha, the identity as git names it, and a class from a
closed list -- spec, amend, approve, launch, record, change, transport,
merge -- derived from who committed it and, for an approval, build, or
verify record, what the record itself says. A summary line follows:
operator acts (approve, amend, launch, transport, merge), product acts
(record), executor commits (change), stops (non-pass records), and
since (the product version on the first record). A build or verify
record's own commit, from before v0.18.0 gave the driver and the
executor their own identities, prints as record-or-launch with a note,
counted toward neither total, rather than guessed. --json prints the
rows and the summary as one object. See issue #128's lane for the full
rules it enforces.

stops reads, never writes. For item N (or every item under
paths.records when N is omitted), one row per non-pass build or verify
record: the record, its stop's class, provenance, and detail, and the
answerer -- "counsel:<verdict>" when a counsel record read this exact
stop, else "person" for a question, else "guard" for a class a guard
alone raised (GUARDED_STOP_CLASSES), else "none". A record too old to
carry stop at all prints the doctor's own phrase for it, "before stop
was recorded", and is counted there rather than by class. Totals per
class (read from the build record schema's own enum, never retyped) and
per answerer, plus the before-stop count, follow the rows. --json prints
the rows and the totals as one object. See issue #129's lane for the
full rules it enforces.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import fnmatch
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time
import tomllib
from datetime import datetime, timezone
from pathlib import Path

MANIFEST = "attavo.toml"
STAGES = ("spec", "approval", "build", "verify")
CRITERION_TIMEOUT = 120
RECORD_RE = re.compile(r"^([a-z]+)-([0-9]{2,})\.json$")
BUILD_ITERATION_BUDGET = 3
BUILD_HALT_EXIT = 3
CHECKS_FEEDBACK_TAIL_LINES = 200
PRODUCT_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
# issue #84: a constant the lane names, not a manifest key, until a tenant
# disagrees -- how long drive gives a forge's checks room to conclude
# before it gives up and stops rather than call a judge on a tree the
# forge has not yet finished reading. Tenant zero's own checks conclude
# about five minutes after a push; the default bound gives them room to
# finish so a person is not the one re-running drive after CI, which is
# exactly the transport act this lane retires. ATTAVO_DRIVE_POLL_BOUND_S
# and ATTAVO_DRIVE_POLL_INTERVAL_S, read at call time rather than once at
# import, are for a test's own clock, not a tenant's -- there is no
# manifest key for either.
DRIVE_FORGE_POLL_BOUND_DEFAULT_S = 600.0
DRIVE_FORGE_POLL_INTERVAL_DEFAULT_S = 20.0


def drive_forge_poll_bound_s() -> float:
    return float(os.environ.get("ATTAVO_DRIVE_POLL_BOUND_S", DRIVE_FORGE_POLL_BOUND_DEFAULT_S))


def drive_forge_poll_interval_s() -> float:
    return float(os.environ.get("ATTAVO_DRIVE_POLL_INTERVAL_S", DRIVE_FORGE_POLL_INTERVAL_DEFAULT_S))


class Proof:
    def __init__(self, key: str, title: str) -> None:
        self.key = key
        self.title = title
        self.holds = False
        self.reason = ""
        self.extra: dict = {}

    def fail(self, reason: str, **extra) -> "Proof":
        self.holds, self.reason = False, reason
        self.extra.update(extra)
        return self

    def hold(self, reason: str, **extra) -> "Proof":
        self.holds, self.reason = True, reason
        self.extra.update(extra)
        return self

    def as_dict(self) -> dict:
        return {"proof": self.key, "title": self.title,
                "holds": self.holds, "reason": self.reason, **self.extra}


def read_manifest(root: Path) -> dict | None:
    path = root / MANIFEST
    if not path.is_file():
        return None
    with path.open("rb") as fh:
        return tomllib.load(fh)


def git(root: Path, *args: str, env: dict | None = None) -> str:
    out = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(root), *args],
                         capture_output=True, text=True, check=False, env=env)
    if out.returncode != 0:
        raise RuntimeError(out.stderr.strip() or "git failed")
    return out.stdout


def product_version() -> str:
    """What the running program is. Three fallbacks, in order, and never an
    exception -- this runs inside write_record, after the work it
    describes already happened, and a raise here would lose the record
    for an invocation that already ran:

      1. ATTAVO_PRODUCT_VERSION, when the environment sets it. The forge
         action sets this from its own ref (action.yml, not here -- this
         function stays forge-agnostic).
      2. git describe --tags --always --dirty against PRODUCT_ROOT: a
         tagged release reports its tag, a checkout reports its commit,
         a dirty tree says so.
      3. "unknown", when PRODUCT_ROOT is not a git repository at all --
         the case for a forge that extracts this action from a tarball
         rather than a git checkout, which is exactly when (1) is
         expected to already have supplied the answer.

    Distinct from the tenant's product_pin -- an operator running a
    checkout, which is how every build and verify have actually been run
    so far, bypasses the pin entirely (issue #49)."""
    env = os.environ.get("ATTAVO_PRODUCT_VERSION")
    if env:
        return env
    try:
        return git(PRODUCT_ROOT, "describe", "--tags", "--always", "--dirty").strip()
    except RuntimeError:
        return "unknown"


def now_iso() -> str:
    return datetime.now(timezone.utc).strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")


def derive_item_id(root: Path) -> str | None:
    """ATTAVO_ITEM first, not last: a rejection fixture's own item id must
    win over whatever attavo/N-* branch the enclosing tenant repository
    happens to be checked out on -- the common case is doctor running from
    a real item's own branch, where a fallback-only env var would never be
    consulted at all and every gate fixture would silently resolve to the
    real item instead of its own."""
    env_item = os.environ.get("ATTAVO_ITEM")
    if env_item:
        return env_item
    out = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(root), "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD"],
                         capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    branch = out.stdout.strip()
    m = re.match(r"^attavo/(\d+)-", branch)
    return m.group(1) if m else None


def fixture_item_id(fixture: Path) -> str | None:
    """The one item id under a rejection fixture's own paths.specs, so the
    doctor can export it as ATTAVO_ITEM: a fixture carries no attavo/N-*
    branch for a gate command with no argument to derive from otherwise."""
    fm = read_manifest(fixture)
    specs_rel = ((fm or {}).get("paths") or {}).get("specs")
    if not specs_rel:
        return None
    specs_dir = fixture / specs_rel
    if not specs_dir.is_dir():
        return None
    items = sorted(p.name for p in specs_dir.iterdir() if p.is_dir())
    return items[0] if len(items) == 1 else None


class ShallowCheckoutError(RuntimeError):
    pass


def last_commit_touching(root: Path, relpath: str) -> str | None:
    shallow = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(root), "rev-parse", "--is-shallow-repository"],
                             capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if shallow.stdout.strip() == "true":
        raise ShallowCheckoutError(
            "shallow checkout: git log -1 -- <path> returns HEAD for every path on a "
            "shallow clone; check out with fetch-depth: 0")
    out = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(root), "log", "-1", "--format=%H", "--", relpath],
                         capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    sha = out.stdout.strip()
    return sha or None


# --- record files: <stage>-NN.json, one per attempt, never overwritten ---

SCHEMA_RECORDS_DIR = PRODUCT_ROOT / "schema" / "records"


def stage_schema(stage: str) -> dict:
    """schema/records/<stage>.schema.json -- this repository's own
    declaration of that stage's record shape (issue #60). The only
    artifact the outbound contract needs; there is no accessor for it
    beyond reading the file."""
    return json.loads((SCHEMA_RECORDS_DIR / f"{stage}.schema.json").read_text())


def schema_id_version(schema: dict) -> int:
    """The trailing integer of a schema's $id (".../records/build/2" -> 2)
    -- the single place a record shape's version lives. write_record reads
    this at call time rather than carrying its own copy, so the version it
    stamps cannot drift from what the schema declares (issue #60)."""
    return int(schema["$id"].rsplit("/", 1)[-1])


class RecordItemMismatch(RuntimeError):
    """A record's item field disagrees with the directory it was read
    from (issue #60, acceptance 5) -- a misfiled or copied record, refused
    rather than trusted."""


def _load_record(path: Path, item_id: str) -> dict:
    """The one place in this repository that turns a record file's bytes
    into a dict (issue #60, acceptance 6). Defaults record_version to 1
    when absent -- version 1 is every record written before this release
    and is never migrated -- and fills item from the directory when
    absent; refuses when a present item disagrees with it."""
    data = json.loads(path.read_text())
    data.setdefault("record_version", 1)
    item = data.get("item")
    if item is None:
        data["item"] = item_id
    elif item != item_id:
        raise RecordItemMismatch(
            f"{path}: item {item!r} disagrees with its directory {item_id!r}")
    return data


def parse_attempts(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> dict[int, dict]:
    attempts: dict[int, dict] = {}
    if not records_dir.is_dir():
        return attempts
    for f in records_dir.glob(f"{stage}-*.json"):
        m = RECORD_RE.match(f.name)
        if not m or m.group(1) != stage:
            continue
        try:
            attempts[int(m.group(2))] = _load_record(f, records_dir.name)
        except json.JSONDecodeError:
            continue
    return attempts


def latest_record(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> dict | None:
    attempts = parse_attempts(records_dir, stage)
    return attempts[max(attempts)] if attempts else None


def next_attempt_number(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> int:
    attempts = parse_attempts(records_dir, stage)
    return (max(attempts) + 1) if attempts else 1


def latest_attempt_path(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> Path | None:
    """The path of the highest-numbered <stage>-NN.json, or None."""
    attempts = parse_attempts(records_dir, stage)
    if not attempts:
        return None
    return records_dir / f"{stage}-{max(attempts):02d}.json"


def write_record(records_dir: Path, stage: str, record: dict, m: dict, item: str,
                 log: str | None = None) -> Path:
    """Every record gains record_version (this stage schema's own $id
    version -- issue #60) and item (verbatim from the caller, matching the
    record's directory segment), plus product_version (what ran, derived
    here) and product_pin (what the tenant declared, copied verbatim from
    the manifest, or null when the manifest carries no [attavo] table) --
    the latter two recorded, never enforced (issue #49). A manifest
    missing the table is an absent fact, not a crash: this is the one
    caller of write_record that must not raise on a shape the manifest
    schema forbids but tomllib.load happily returns.

    Refuses (raises ValueError) a record carrying a key this stage's
    schema does not declare -- the declared key set enforced with the
    standard library, not a jsonschema import: bin/attavo is a single
    script a tenant pins, and a runtime dependency changes installation.

    Also refuses a `status: "failed"` record that carries no trail at all
    -- log the empty or missing text it will sit beside, and refusal and
    halted (where the stage's schema has them) both null (issue #53).
    `returned`, `pass`, `halted`, and `refused` owe no such trail. log is
    None for a stage that invoked no adapter (verify's `returned`), which
    write_record then writes no log file for; otherwise the log is
    written before the record, so a refusal leaves no half-pair."""
    records_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    nn = next_attempt_number(records_dir, stage)
    schema = stage_schema(stage)
    record = {**record, "record_version": schema_id_version(schema), "item": item,
              "product_version": product_version(),
              "product_pin": (m.get("attavo") or {}).get("version")}
    undeclared = set(record) - set(schema.get("properties", {}))
    if undeclared:
        raise ValueError(f"write_record: {stage} record carries key(s) its schema does not "
                         f"declare: {', '.join(sorted(undeclared))}")
    if (record.get("status") == "failed" and not log
            and record.get("refusal") is None and record.get("halted") is None):
        raise ValueError(f"write_record: {stage} record status is 'failed' with no log, "
                         f"refusal, or halted to say why")
    path = records_dir / f"{stage}-{nn:02d}.json"
    if log is not None:
        path.with_suffix(".log").write_text(log)
    path.write_text(json.dumps(record, indent=2) + "\n")
    return path


# --- stop: every non-pass build or verify record's class and cause (issue
# #89) -- derived here, at write time, from the refusal, halted, and log
# the caller already has. Never a fresh read of anything: the tenant zero
# specimens this retires (#576 build-11, #659 build-01 and verify-01) are
# exactly a stop the product wrote with nothing beside it to explain
# itself after the fact. ---

STOP_CLASSES = ("non-question", "outlived-invocation", "no-cause", "forge-unconcluded",
               "ceiling", "claims-boundary", "repeated-act", "unclassed", "question",
               "environment", "gate", "died", "hung")
STOP_PROVENANCES = ("product", "tenant", "executor", "spec")

# Beyond the enum spelling itself, the prose issue #88 actually shipped
# (adapters/claude-code/run and adapters/codex/run, on main since
# v0.16.0) once put a class's own name, or a real specimen's wording of
# it, in the transcript for the generic match below to find. Issue #94
# moves non-question and outlived-invocation onto report_class instead
# (below): non-question's own halt line ("halted: class non-question")
# would otherwise still satisfy the bare-name match here with no adapter
# report-line field involved at all, and outlived-invocation's refusal
# never named its class in text to begin with, so both are disabled here
# -- the same pattern issue #93 established for ceiling, just below,
# rather than removed outright.
CLASS_TEXT_MARKERS: dict[str, tuple[str, ...]] = {
    "non-question": (),
    "outlived-invocation": (),
    # Issue #93: ceiling's own text-based check is CEILING_MARKERS, below,
    # with provenance "product" -- a fact about the tool, not an executor
    # judgment like non-question/outlived-invocation are. Left at the
    # default (the class's own name, "ceiling"), the generic match above
    # would instead catch adapters/codex/run's and
    # adapters/claude-code/run's own pre-emptive refusal -- which must
    # carry the word "ceiling" by the same issue's own brief -- as
    # provenance "executor", before CEILING_MARKERS is ever consulted.
    "ceiling": (),
    # A genuine question (issue #100's own followups) is read structurally
    # -- status "halted" carrying a non-empty halted.question -- not by
    # scanning for the literal word "question", which the question's own
    # text very often contains regardless of what it is actually asking.
    # Left at the default, the generic match above would misclassify any
    # question that happens to use the word "question" as executor
    # provenance, before the structural check in derive_stop is reached.
    "question": (),
    # environment/tenant (issue #115) is set directly by cmd_build's own
    # preflight -- the one caller who knows a [checks].command exited 2
    # on the approved tree -- never derived from adapter text. Left at
    # the default, the generic match above would catch the word
    # "environment" anywhere in a log or refusal (e.g. codex's own
    # OPENAI_API_KEY auth-mismatch refusal, adapters/codex/run's
    # check_auth) as an environment/executor stop that never happened.
    "environment": (),
}

# Known tool error text for a declared ceiling -- issue #86's own specimen
# (codex's "Input exceeds..." line on a diff too large to judge) is
# "input_too_large"; a matching line anywhere in the log, not only its
# last line, since cmd_build and cmd_verify both append "[adapter exited
# N]" after whatever the tool itself printed. "ceiling exceeded" is issue
# #93's own addition: both adapters now refuse pre-emptively, before the
# tool is ever invoked, against a declared or fixed input limit, and
# their refusal text is not caught by any of the three markers above.
CEILING_MARKERS = ("input_too_large", "exceeds the maximum context length",
                  "context_length_exceeded", "ceiling exceeded")


# Issue #100's followups: an unclassed detail is the log's last line
# verbatim, and that line is sometimes an adapter's own event JSON or a
# tool's full error text -- thousands of characters wide. Capped here,
# at write time, so a filed issue's own summary of it (file-stops'
# log_tail already carries the full picture) stays short; the class
# itself, "unclassed", already says the full explanation lives in the
# log, not in this field.
UNCLASSED_DETAIL_MAX_CHARS = 200


def _log_last_line(log: str | None) -> str:
    lines = [l for l in (log or "").splitlines() if l.strip()]
    last = lines[-1] if lines else ""
    return last[:UNCLASSED_DETAIL_MAX_CHARS]


def _carries_a_known_class(text: str | None) -> str | None:
    """Whichever of STOP_CLASSES this text names -- its own spelling, or
    one of CLASS_TEXT_MARKERS' real-world phrasings of it, when that
    class is not disabled there -- or None. Issue #88 is where an
    adapter's own halt or refusal first named its class this way; issue
    #93 added ceiling's own pre-emptive refusal to that set and disabled
    its bare-name match (CEILING_MARKERS covers it instead); issue #94
    disables non-question and outlived-invocation the same way, reading
    both from the adapter's own report line (derive_stop's report_class)
    now instead."""
    text = text or ""
    for name in STOP_CLASSES:
        markers = CLASS_TEXT_MARKERS.get(name, (name,))
        if any(marker in text for marker in markers):
            return name
    return None


def derive_stop(status: str, *, refusal: str | None = None, halted: dict | None = None,
                log: str | None = None, report_class: str | None = None,
                refusal_provenance: str | None = None) -> dict | None:
    """The stop field for a build or verify record: None for status "pass",
    else {class, provenance, detail} from what the caller already knows.

      - report_class -- payload.get("class") off the adapter's own report
        line (issue #88, #94), when it names a STOP_CLASSES member -- is
        that class, provenance "executor". build.schema.json and
        verify.schema.json stay untouched by issue #88 on purpose, so the
        class lives only on the report line, never a structured field
        cmd_build or cmd_verify forwards on its own account; the caller
        reads payload itself and passes this in, since only it still has
        payload once an iteration ends. This is non-question's and
        outlived-invocation's only path to a record now (issue #94):
        CLASS_TEXT_MARKERS disables both for the text scan below, the
        same way issue #93 disabled ceiling's bare-name match there. A
        class an adapter does not yet put on its report line --
        outlived-invocation today, since that refusal path prints no
        report line at all -- falls to unclassed below, same as any
        other unnamed cause, until the adapter that raises it puts a
        class on the line the way non-question already does.
      - a halt carrying a non-empty halted.question, once it is not a
        non-question caught above, is a genuine question -- question/spec,
        with the question's own first line as detail (issue #100's
        followups). This is the specimen issue #100 itself was built to
        retire: tenant zero's #663 build-01, a proper question, classed
        unclassed/product and filed as a product defect with the wrong
        provenance -- a question is about the definition of done, which is
        a person's or a ruling's to answer, never the product's own
        mechanism.
      - failing that, a refusal or the log itself that already names its
        class in text (issue #88, #93 for ceiling's own pre-emptive
        refusal) is that class, provenance "executor" -- the log is
        checked because neither adapter's refusal *field* carries the
        class today; the class-naming line each adapter prints reaches
        only the transcript
      - a refusal that is not that -- the write-outside-claims path
        itself, not the "commit ... not authored by the executor" shape
        -- is claims-boundary/spec, unless the caller already knows this
        refusal touched paths.records or paths.specs (issue #140:
        protected_path_touched, checked ahead of the ordinary claims
        match in cmd_build) and passes refusal_provenance="executor" --
        the product's own directories are not a claim a spec makes, so a
        refusal there is not the spec's boundary to answer for
      - a returned verify (the forge was not green; no adapter ran) is
        forge-unconcluded/tenant
      - an adapter exit whose log carries a declared-ceiling error line is
        ceiling/product
      - anything else is unclassed/product, with the log's last line
        (capped at UNCLASSED_DETAIL_MAX_CHARS) as its detail -- the other
        class that carries a detail, since every remaining class's
        evidence already lives in refusal, halted, forge_checks, or the
        log itself.
    """
    if status == "pass":
        return None
    if status == "returned":
        return {"class": "forge-unconcluded", "provenance": "tenant", "detail": None}
    if report_class in STOP_CLASSES:
        return {"class": report_class, "provenance": "executor", "detail": None}
    question = (halted or {}).get("question")
    if status == "halted" and question:
        return {"class": "question", "provenance": "spec",
               "detail": question.splitlines()[0]}
    known = _carries_a_known_class(refusal) or _carries_a_known_class(log)
    if known:
        return {"class": known, "provenance": "executor", "detail": None}
    if status == "refused" and refusal is not None and not refusal.startswith("commit "):
        return {"class": "claims-boundary", "provenance": refusal_provenance or "spec",
               "detail": None}
    if log and any(marker in log.lower() for marker in CEILING_MARKERS):
        return {"class": "ceiling", "provenance": "product", "detail": None}
    return {"class": "unclassed", "provenance": "product", "detail": _log_last_line(log)}


def gate_stop(refusal: str | None) -> dict:
    """The stop for a refused spec-gate record: class "gate", provenance
    "spec", the refusal string itself as cause (issue #137) -- not
    derive_stop's generic claims-boundary/spec fallback issue #133
    reused, which named a boundary the record never crossed: "criterion
    fails against the tree plus stub: doctor-template-absent.sh" is not
    a write outside claims. cmd_spec_gate is untouched by this lane, so
    a spec record still carries no stop field of its own; this is
    synthesized wherever a refused one is read (item_stops,
    cmd_file_stops, proof_no_stop_lacks_cause), never persisted."""
    return {"class": "gate", "provenance": "spec", "detail": refusal}


def load_records(records_dir: Path) -> dict[str, dict[str, dict]]:
    """{item_id: {stage: latest_record}} for every <records>/<item>/<stage>-NN.json."""
    items: dict[str, dict[str, dict]] = {}
    if not records_dir.is_dir():
        return items
    for item_dir in sorted(p for p in records_dir.iterdir() if p.is_dir()):
        by_stage: dict[str, dict[int, dict]] = {}
        for rec in sorted(item_dir.glob("*.json")):
            m = RECORD_RE.match(rec.name)
            if not m:
                continue
            stage, n = m.group(1), int(m.group(2))
            try:
                data = _load_record(rec, item_dir.name)
            except json.JSONDecodeError:
                continue
            by_stage.setdefault(stage, {})[n] = data
        items[item_dir.name] = {stage: attempts[max(attempts)] for stage, attempts in by_stage.items()}
    return items


# --- doctor's three proofs ---

def proof_every_gate_rejects(root: Path, m: dict | None) -> Proof:
    p = Proof("every-gate-rejects", "every gate rejects its fixture")
    if m is None:
        return p.fail(f"no {MANIFEST} at {root}; no gate is registered")
    gates = m.get("gates") or {}
    if not gates:
        return p.fail("[gates] is empty; a gate that has rejected nothing does not exist")
    fixtures = root / m.get("paths", {}).get("rejections", "")
    # Proposal, not yet ruled on (issue #54): a gate that retires nothing
    # only adds, so proof one reports it here rather than waiting on the
    # schema alone -- read_manifest never validates against the manifest
    # schema at runtime, so a schema requirement is not an enforced one.
    no_retires = [name for name, gate in sorted(gates.items()) if not gate.get("retires")]
    missing, accepted = [], []
    for name, gate in sorted(gates.items()):
        fixture = fixtures / name
        if not fixture.is_dir():
            missing.append(name)
            continue
        env = dict(os.environ)
        item = fixture_item_id(fixture)
        if item is not None:
            env["ATTAVO_ITEM"] = item
        run = subprocess.run(gate["command"], shell=True, cwd=str(fixture),
                             capture_output=True, text=True, check=False, env=env)
        if run.returncode == 0:
            accepted.append(name)
    if missing:
        return p.fail(f"gate(s) with no rejection fixture: {', '.join(missing)}")
    if accepted:
        return p.fail(f"gate(s) that accepted their rejection fixture: {', '.join(accepted)}")
    if no_retires:
        return p.fail(f"gate(s) with no retires: {', '.join(no_retires)}")
    return p.hold(f"{len(gates)} gate(s) rejected their fixture")


def _merges_reachable(root: Path) -> tuple[list[dict], str | None]:
    """(merges, refusal): every merge commit on the default branch's own
    first-parent line -- sha, parents, and its own first-parent diff --
    read by one process rather than the git log plus one git diff per
    merge merge_introducing_records used to pay walking toward the one
    it wants (issue #153). refs/remotes/origin/HEAD names the default
    branch without hard-coding "main", the same primitive
    diff_since_approval already uses (issue #52); walking from it rather
    than from HEAD is what keeps a transport merge on an item branch, a
    pull_request checkout's own synthetic test merge, and a merge_group's
    temporary merge from ever being mistaken for an item's merge (issue
    #169) -- none of those is reachable from the default branch's first-
    parent line by definition. When refs/remotes/origin/HEAD is not set,
    this refuses by name rather than guess, exactly as diff_since_approval
    does.

    The walk starts at HEAD itself when the current checkout is literally
    on the branch refs/remotes/origin/HEAD names (an ordinary checkout of
    the default branch, on push or on a developer's own machine) and at
    the remote-tracking ref's own value otherwise (any other branch, or
    the detached HEAD a pull_request or merge_group checkout leaves
    behind) -- HEAD only ever *is* the default branch's first-parent line
    in the former case; in the latter, only the remote-tracking ref names
    it. --diff-merges=first-parent gives each merge's diff against its
    first parent in the same pass git log --merges already makes to list
    them, in the same order, newest first."""
    symref = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "-C", str(root), "symbolic-ref", "-q", "refs/remotes/origin/HEAD"],
        capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if symref.returncode != 0:
        return [], "refs/remotes/origin/HEAD is not set; run `git remote set-head origin -a`"
    default_remote_ref = symref.stdout.strip()
    default_branch = default_remote_ref.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
    current_branch = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "-C", str(root), "symbolic-ref", "-q", "--short", "HEAD"],
        capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    default_ref = ("HEAD" if current_branch.returncode == 0
                   and current_branch.stdout.strip() == default_branch
                   else default_remote_ref)
    try:
        out = git(root, "log", default_ref, "--first-parent", "--merges",
                 "--diff-merges=first-parent", "--name-only",
                 "--format=\x01%H\x1f%P")
    except RuntimeError:
        return [], None
    merges = []
    for block in out.split("\x01")[1:]:
        header, _, rest = block.partition("\n")
        sha, parents = header.split("\x1f")
        merges.append({"sha": sha, "parents": parents.split(),
                       "touched": [line for line in rest.splitlines() if line.strip()]})
    return merges, None


def merge_introducing_records(root: Path, records_rel: str, item: str,
                              stages: tuple[str, ...],
                              merges: list[dict] | None = None) -> tuple[str | None, str | None]:
    """(merge_sha, refusal): the merge commit on the default branch's own
    first-parent line whose first-parent diff introduces a record for
    every one of stages, under records_rel/item/ -- the same derivation
    attest itself uses (derive_attest_items), read here so proof two
    requires an item's stage records to have arrived by an actual merge
    on the default branch, not merely to sit in the tree, and not by a
    transport merge, a pull_request checkout's synthetic test merge, or a
    merge_group's temporary merge (issue #69 acceptance row 4; issue
    #169). merge_sha is None with no refusal when no such merge exists
    yet -- the item is not merged, not that the walk failed.

    merges, when given, is _merges_reachable's own result -- ledger's
    cmd_ledger computes it once and passes it to every item rather than
    walking the default branch's merges again for each (issue #153); a
    caller that already has merges has also already handled
    _merges_reachable's refusal, so this never re-derives one. Left
    None, a caller with just the one item to ask (doctor's proof two)
    gets merges computed here, refusal and all."""
    if merges is None:
        merges, refusal = _merges_reachable(root)
        if refusal:
            return None, refusal
    prefix = f"{records_rel.rstrip('/')}/{item}/"
    for merge in merges:
        found: set[str] = set()
        for p in merge["touched"]:
            if p.startswith(prefix):
                m = RECORD_RE.match(Path(p).name)
                if m:
                    found.add(m.group(1))
        if all(s in found for s in stages):
            return merge["sha"], None
    return None, None


INVOCATION_MARKER_SUFFIX = ".started"


def invocation_marker_path(records_dir: Path, stage: str, nn: int) -> Path:
    return records_dir / f"{stage}-{nn:02d}{INVOCATION_MARKER_SUFFIX}"


def write_invocation_marker(root: Path, records_dir: Path, stage: str, nn: int) -> Path:
    """The fact that an invocation began, written before it does: at,
    host, pid, the sha it starts on, and the product that started it.
    Its record's arrival is what retires it; a marker with no record is
    an invocation that died or hangs, and proof five names it."""
    import os
    import socket
    from datetime import datetime, timezone
    records_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    path = invocation_marker_path(records_dir, stage, nn)
    sha = git(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD").strip()
    info = {"at": datetime.now(timezone.utc).strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ"),
            "host": socket.gethostname(), "pid": os.getpid(), "sha": sha,
            "product_version": product_version()}
    path.write_text(json.dumps(info, indent=2) + "\n")
    return path


def invocation_alive(info: dict) -> bool:
    """Whether the marker's pid is a live process on this host. Another
    host's pid is unknowable from here and reads as not alive: a drive
    that ran elsewhere and wrote no record is died by absence."""
    import os
    import socket
    if info.get("host") != socket.gethostname():
        return False
    pid = info.get("pid")
    if not isinstance(pid, int) or isinstance(pid, bool) or pid <= 0:
        return False
    try:
        os.kill(pid, 0)
    except ProcessLookupError:
        return False
    except PermissionError:
        return True
    return True


def invocations_without_record(item_records_dir: Path) -> list[tuple[Path, dict]]:
    """Every <stage>-NN.started under one item whose <stage>-NN.json has
    not arrived, with the marker's own dict ({} when unreadable)."""
    out: list[tuple[Path, dict]] = []
    if not item_records_dir.is_dir():
        return out
    for marker in sorted(item_records_dir.glob(f"*{INVOCATION_MARKER_SUFFIX}")):
        if (item_records_dir / f"{marker.stem}.json").is_file():
            continue
        try:
            with marker.open() as fh:
                info = json.load(fh)
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            info = {}
        out.append((marker, info if isinstance(info, dict) else {}))
    return out


def stall_bound_s(m: dict) -> int | None:
    """[drive].stall_after_s: the tenant's own bound past which a live
    invocation with no new event is hung. A bound the tenant did not
    write is a forecast, so there is no default."""
    bound = (m.get("drive") or {}).get("stall_after_s")
    if isinstance(bound, bool) or not isinstance(bound, (int, float)) or bound <= 0:
        return None
    return int(bound)


def proof_no_invocation_lacks_record(root: Path, m: dict | None) -> Proof:
    p = Proof("no-invocation-lacks-record", "no invocation lacks its record")
    if m is None:
        return p.fail(f"no {MANIFEST} at {root}; no records directory is declared")
    records_rel = m.get("paths", {}).get("records", "")
    records_dir = root / records_rel
    if not records_dir.is_dir():
        return p.fail(f"no records directory under {records_dir}")
    if stall_bound_s(m) is None:
        return p.fail("no [drive].stall_after_s in the manifest; a hung invocation cannot be "
                      "told from a running one")
    recorded = 0
    running: list[str] = []
    problems: list[str] = []
    for item_dir in sorted(d for d in records_dir.iterdir() if d.is_dir()):
        for marker in item_dir.glob(f"*{INVOCATION_MARKER_SUFFIX}"):
            if (item_dir / f"{marker.stem}.json").is_file():
                recorded += 1
        for marker, info in invocations_without_record(item_dir):
            name = f"item {item_dir.name} {marker.stem}"
            if invocation_alive(info):
                running.append(f"{name} (pid {info.get('pid')} since {info.get('at')})")
                continue
            problems.append(f"{name} started {info.get('at')} on {info.get('host')} "
                            f"pid {info.get('pid')} and never wrote its record")
    if problems:
        return p.fail("; ".join(problems), unrecorded_invocations=len(problems),
                      running_invocations=running)
    return p.hold(f"{recorded} invocation(s) recorded, {len(running)} running",
                  unrecorded_invocations=0, running_invocations=running)


def _events_path_for(root: Path, stage: str, nn: int) -> Path | None:
    """The adapter's own events file for this invocation, under the
    checkout's git dir (a worktree's own dir when the checkout is one)."""
    try:
        gitdir = Path(git(root, "rev-parse", "--git-dir").strip())
    except RuntimeError:
        return None
    if not gitdir.is_absolute():
        gitdir = root / gitdir
    path = gitdir / "attavo" / f"{stage}-{nn:02d}.events.jsonl"
    return path if path.is_file() else None


def _events_tail(path: Path | None, lines: int = 20) -> tuple[str, str | None]:
    """(the last lines as a log, the last event's own at)."""
    if path is None:
        return "no events file for this invocation\n", None
    try:
        text = path.read_text()
    except OSError:
        return "events file unreadable\n", None
    tail = text.splitlines()[-lines:]
    last_at = None
    for line in reversed(tail):
        parsed = _parse_json_line(line)
        if not isinstance(parsed, dict):
            continue
        at = parsed.get("at")
        if isinstance(at, str):
            last_at = at
            break
    return "\n".join(tail) + "\n", last_at


def _seconds_since(at: str | None) -> float | None:
    from datetime import datetime, timezone
    if not at:
        return None
    try:
        then = datetime.strptime(at, "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ").replace(tzinfo=timezone.utc)
    except ValueError:
        return None
    return (datetime.now(timezone.utc) - then).total_seconds()


def _drive_recover(root: Path, m: dict, item_id: str) -> int:
    """`attavo drive --recover [N]`: every invocation of item N that began
    and wrote no record ends in one here -- class died when its pid is
    gone or on another host, class hung when it is alive past
    [drive].stall_after_s with no newer event (and terminated); provenance
    product; the events tail as the log. A live invocation inside the
    bound is running and left alone. The record is committed and pushed
    the way every stage record is; the ordinary relaunch then reads a
    non-pass record and does what it does."""
    import os
    import signal
    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    records_dir = root / records_rel / item_id
    bound = stall_bound_s(m)
    if bound is None:
        print("attavo drive: refused: no [drive].stall_after_s in the manifest; a hung "
              "invocation cannot be told from a running one")
        return 1
    pending = invocations_without_record(records_dir)
    if not pending:
        print(f"attavo drive: item {item_id}: every invocation has its record; nothing to recover")
        return 0
    branch = git(root, "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD").strip()
    try:
        git(root, "remote", "get-url", "origin")
        has_origin = True
    except RuntimeError:
        has_origin = False
    recovered = 0
    for marker, info in pending:
        stage, nn_text = marker.stem.rsplit("-", 1)
        nn = int(nn_text)
        events = _events_path_for(root, stage, nn)
        log, last_at = _events_tail(events)
        age = _seconds_since(last_at or info.get("at"))
        pid, host = info.get("pid"), info.get("host")
        if invocation_alive(info):
            if age is None or age <= bound:
                print(f"attavo drive: {marker.stem} is running (pid {pid}, last event "
                      f"{'unknown' if age is None else int(age)} s ago, bound {bound} s); "
                      f"nothing to recover")
                continue
            cls = "hung"
            try:
                os.kill(int(pid), signal.SIGTERM)
                ended = "terminated"
            except OSError as exc:
                ended = f"not terminated ({exc})"
            detail = (f"pid {pid} on {host} alive with no event for {int(age)} s "
                      f"(bound {bound} s); {ended}")
        else:
            cls = "died"
            detail = (f"pid {pid} on {host} is not running and wrote no {marker.stem}.json; "
                      f"started {info.get('at')}, last event {last_at or 'none'}")
        if next_attempt_number(records_dir, stage) != nn:
            print(f"attavo drive: stopped: {marker.name} names attempt {nn} but the next "
                  f"{stage} record would be {next_attempt_number(records_dir, stage):02d}; "
                  f"a later record already exists, read the records before recovering")
            return 1
        schema = stage_schema(stage)
        record = {k: None for k in schema.get("required", [])}
        approval = latest_record(records_dir, "approval") or {}
        record.update({"sha": info.get("sha"), "start_sha": info.get("sha"),
                       "approval_sha": approval.get("sha"), "at": info.get("at"),
                       "adapter": (m.get("executor") or {}).get(stage),
                       "status": "failed", "refusal": None, "halted": None,
                       "stop": {"class": cls, "provenance": "product", "detail": detail}})
        if "wall_clock_s" in record:
            record["wall_clock_s"] = None if age is None else int(age)
        record = {k: v for k, v in record.items() if k in schema.get("properties", {})}
        try:
            path = write_record(records_dir, stage, record, m, item_id, log=log)
        except ValueError as exc:
            print(f"attavo drive: stopped: could not write the {cls} record for {marker.stem}: {exc}")
            return 1
        try:
            commit_record(root, [path], f"attavo: {stage} record for item {item_id} "
                                        f"({path.stem}), recovered as {cls}")
        except RuntimeError as exc:
            print(f"attavo drive: stopped: could not commit the {cls} record: {exc}")
            return 1
        if has_origin:
            ok, err = push_record_commit(root, branch)
            if not ok:
                print(f"attavo drive: stopped: could not push the {cls} record: {err}")
                return 1
        else:
            print(f"attavo drive: note: no origin remote; {path.name} is committed, not pushed")
        print(f"attavo drive: recovered {marker.stem} as {cls}: {detail}")
        recovered += 1
    print(f"attavo drive: item {item_id}: {recovered} invocation(s) recovered")
    return 0


def proof_one_item_every_stage(root: Path, m: dict | None) -> Proof:
    p = Proof("one-item-every-stage", "one item has traversed every stage")
    if m is None:
        return p.fail(f"no {MANIFEST} at {root}; no records directory is declared")
    records_rel = m.get("paths", {}).get("records", "")
    records_dir = root / records_rel
    items = load_records(records_dir)
    if not items:
        return p.fail(f"no work item under {records_dir.relative_to(root)}")
    complete = [item for item, stages in items.items() if all(s in stages for s in STAGES)]
    if not complete:
        best = max(items.items(), key=lambda kv: len(kv[1]))
        lacking = [s for s in STAGES if s not in best[1]]
        return p.fail(f"no item carries every stage; item {best[0]} lacks {', '.join(lacking)}")

    # issue #74: a complete item is counted only when its chain holds --
    # attavo attest refuses a build re-approved after the fact or a
    # verify whose build went stale, and the coherence gate refuses the
    # merge group on exactly that; a proof that never asked would call a
    # traversal the gate itself would not let through. The refusal text
    # is check_attest_chain's own, never re-typed here.
    refused, held, unmerged = [], [], []
    for item in complete:
        chain, message = check_attest_chain(records_dir / item, item)
        if chain is None:
            refused.append(f"item {item}: {message}")
            continue
        merge_sha, merge_refusal = merge_introducing_records(root, records_rel, item, STAGES)
        if merge_refusal:
            return p.fail(merge_refusal)
        if merge_sha:
            held.append((item, merge_sha))
        else:
            unmerged.append(item)

    if held:
        names = ", ".join(item for item, _ in held)
        shas = ", ".join(sha[:12] for _, sha in held)
        return p.hold(f"item(s) {names} carry {', '.join(STAGES)} with the chain held, "
                      f"merged at {shas}")
    if refused:
        return p.fail("; ".join(refused))
    return p.fail(f"item(s) {', '.join(unmerged)} carry every stage with the chain held but no "
                  f"merge commit's first-parent diff introduces all of them")


def commit_subject(root: Path, sha: str) -> str:
    return git(root, "log", "-1", "--format=%s", sha).strip()


def touches_boundary(root: Path, sha: str, boundary_paths: list[str]) -> bool:
    """Whether sha's first-parent diff touches a path [spec.boundary].paths
    declares governed -- an ordinary merge that does is a boundary
    evasion (issue #72): the tenant carried a governed change around the
    path the manifest itself names."""
    diff = git(root, "diff", "--name-only", f"{sha}^1", sha).splitlines()
    return any(fnmatch.fnmatch(path, pattern) for path in diff for pattern in boundary_paths)


def proof_no_commit_lacks_record(root: Path, m: dict | None) -> Proof:
    p = Proof("no-commit-lacks-record", "no commit lacks a record")
    if m is None:
        return p.fail(f"no {MANIFEST} at {root}; the tenant has no record boundary")
    try:
        install = git(root, "log", "--diff-filter=A", "--format=%H",
                      "--reverse", "--", MANIFEST).split()
    except RuntimeError as exc:
        return p.fail(f"git: {exc}")
    if not install:
        return p.fail(f"{MANIFEST} is not committed; the tenant has no record boundary")
    install_sha = install[0]
    full_chain = git(root, "rev-list", f"{install_sha}..HEAD", "--first-parent").split()
    if not full_chain:
        return p.fail(f"no commit after the install commit {install_sha[:12]}; nothing has been recorded")

    # issue #74: [doctor] from lets a tenant declare the doctor's read to
    # start at a later commit on main's first-parent line -- the point
    # after which every push arrived through the queue -- rather than the
    # install commit, which on a repository that adopted the product
    # after its history began is a boundary the doctor cannot otherwise
    # see. Any spelling git resolves is accepted (a short sha, a tag, a
    # ref) -- resolved with rev-parse --verify before the first-parent
    # check runs, so the two failure modes stay distinct: a spelling that
    # names no commit at all, and a commit that names one off the line.
    # Checked, not trusted: neither is silently forgiven back to install.
    declared_from = (m.get("doctor") or {}).get("from")
    window_start = install_sha
    if declared_from is not None:
        try:
            resolved_from = git(root, "rev-parse", "--verify", f"{declared_from}^{{commit}}").strip()
        except RuntimeError:
            return p.fail(f"[doctor] from {declared_from} does not resolve to a commit")
        if resolved_from != install_sha and resolved_from not in full_chain:
            return p.fail(f"[doctor] from {declared_from} is not on main's first-parent "
                          f"line after the install commit {install_sha[:12]}")
        window_start = resolved_from

    if window_start == install_sha:
        window_spec = f"{install_sha}..HEAD"
        commits = full_chain
    else:
        window_spec = f"{window_start}^..HEAD"
        commits = git(root, "rev-list", window_spec, "--first-parent").split()
    before_window = len(full_chain) - len(commits)

    records_rel = m.get("paths", {}).get("records", "")
    records_dir = root / records_rel
    named: set[str] = set()
    for stages in load_records(records_dir).values():
        for rec in stages.values():
            sha = rec.get("sha")
            if isinstance(sha, str):
                named.add(sha)
            for c in rec.get("commits", []) or []:
                if isinstance(c, str):
                    named.add(c)
    record_commits = set(git(root, "rev-list", window_spec, "--first-parent",
                             "--", records_rel).split()) if records_rel else set()
    merges = set(git(root, "rev-list", window_spec, "--first-parent", "--merges").split())
    boundary_paths = ((m.get("spec") or {}).get("boundary") or {}).get("paths") or []

    # Every first-parent commit in the window is exactly one of three
    # (issue #72): recorded (a record names it, or its diff touches
    # paths.records), ordinary (a merge that doesn't), or unrecorded
    # (anything else -- on a protected main with a queue, a non-merge
    # commit is the only way a change arrives without the forge).
    recorded, ordinary, unrecorded = [], [], []
    ordinary_commits, unrecorded_commits = [], []
    boundary_evasions = 0
    for c in commits:
        if c in named or c in record_commits:
            recorded.append(c)
        elif c in merges:
            ordinary.append(c)
            evasion = bool(boundary_paths and touches_boundary(root, c, boundary_paths))
            if evasion:
                boundary_evasions += 1
            ordinary_commits.append({"sha": c, "subject": commit_subject(root, c),
                                     "boundary_evasion": evasion})
        else:
            unrecorded.append(c)
            unrecorded_commits.append({"sha": c, "subject": commit_subject(root, c)})

    counts = f"{len(recorded)} recorded, {len(ordinary)} ordinary, {len(unrecorded)} unrecorded"
    evasions = boundary_evasions if boundary_paths else "n/a"
    extra = {"recorded": len(recorded), "ordinary": len(ordinary),
             "unrecorded": len(unrecorded), "boundary_evasions": evasions,
             "before_window": before_window,
             "ordinary_commits": ordinary_commits, "unrecorded_commits": unrecorded_commits}
    if unrecorded:
        return p.fail(f"{unrecorded[-1][:12]} lacks a record; {counts}; boundary evasions: "
                      f"{evasions}; before the window: {before_window}", **extra)
    return p.hold(f"{counts}; boundary evasions: {evasions}; before the window: {before_window}",
                  **extra)


ADAPTER_EXIT_RE = re.compile(r"^\[adapter exited \d+\]$")

_JSON_LINE_PARSE_FAILED = object()


def _parse_json_line(text: str):
    """A line's own parsed JSON value, or the sentinel
    _JSON_LINE_PARSE_FAILED when it does not parse -- the one call site
    in this file for a defensive, line-at-a-time JSON parse, shared by
    proof four's own tool-error check below (any valid JSON satisfies
    it) and an events line's own `at` read (issue #151, which further
    requires a dict). A dedicated sentinel, not None: a line that is
    the literal JSON value null parses successfully and must read as
    such, not as a failure."""
    try:
        return json.loads(text)
    except ValueError:
        return _JSON_LINE_PARSE_FAILED


def _log_owes_tool_error(log: str | None) -> bool:
    """True when a log's last non-empty line is exactly an adapter-exit
    marker and every line before it parses as JSON -- an adapter's own
    event stream, never a tool's own words (issue #94's own specimen,
    tenant zero's attest/records/659/verify-01.log: codex's
    "thread.started" event, then nothing but "[adapter exited 1]" -- the
    tool's real error line dropped before this reached the log, issue
    #86's own bug in split_adapter_stdout). A log that is only the exit
    marker, nothing before it at all, still owes the same line."""
    if not log:
        return False
    lines = [l for l in log.splitlines() if l.strip()]
    if not lines or not ADAPTER_EXIT_RE.match(lines[-1]):
        return False
    for l in lines[:-1]:
        if _parse_json_line(l) is _JSON_LINE_PARSE_FAILED:
            return False
    return True


def proof_no_stop_lacks_cause(root: Path, m: dict | None) -> Proof:
    p = Proof("no-stop-lacks-cause", "no stop lacks its cause")
    if m is None:
        return p.fail(f"no {MANIFEST} at {root}; no records directory is declared")
    records_rel = m.get("paths", {}).get("records", "")
    records_dir = root / records_rel
    if not records_dir.is_dir():
        return p.fail(f"no records directory under {records_dir}")

    # issue #89's own window lesson, proof three's already: a record
    # before record_version 3 has no stop by construction (schema
    # versions 1 and 2 predate it) -- counted here, never refused.
    older = 0
    checked = 0
    problems: list[str] = []
    for item_dir in sorted(d for d in records_dir.iterdir() if d.is_dir()):
        for stage in STOP_STAGES:
            for n, rec in sorted(parse_attempts(item_dir, stage).items()):
                if rec.get("record_version", 1) < 3:
                    older += 1
                    continue
                if rec.get("status") == "pass":
                    continue
                checked += 1
                name = f"{stage}-{n:02d}"
                stop = rec.get("stop")
                if not stop:
                    problems.append(f"item {item_dir.name} {name} carries no stop")
                    continue
                cls, provenance = stop.get("class"), stop.get("provenance")
                if cls not in STOP_CLASSES:
                    problems.append(f"item {item_dir.name} {name} stop.class {cls!r} names "
                                    f"no enumerated class")
                    continue
                if provenance not in STOP_PROVENANCES:
                    problems.append(f"item {item_dir.name} {name} stop.provenance "
                                    f"{provenance!r} names no enumerated provenance")
                    continue
                if cls == "unclassed" and not stop.get("detail"):
                    problems.append(f"item {item_dir.name} {name} is unclassed with no detail")
                    continue
                log_path = item_dir / f"{name}.log"
                log = log_path.read_text() if log_path.is_file() else None
                # Issue #89, rejection test 1's own specimen (tenant
                # zero's #659 build-01): a failed build that actually ran
                # the gates -- selected_tests is set only once cmd_build
                # reaches them -- owes their heading in the log, whatever
                # else stop already says.
                if (stage == "build" and rec.get("status") == "failed"
                        and rec.get("selected_tests") is not None
                        and "--- attavo gates, iteration" not in (log or "")):
                    problems.append(f"item {item_dir.name} {name} failed after the gates ran "
                                    f"but its log carries no gates heading")
                    continue
                if _log_owes_tool_error(log):
                    problems.append(f"item {item_dir.name} {name}'s log ends in an adapter "
                                    f"exit with nothing before it but event lines; the tool's "
                                    f"own error line is owed")

        # issue #137: a refused spec-gate record is a stop too, class
        # "gate" -- synthesized the same way item_stops and
        # cmd_file_stops already read it, since cmd_spec_gate itself
        # carries no stop field to check for one. No record_version
        # gate here: unlike build/verify's own stop field, gate is never
        # persisted, so there is no older shape that predates it.
        for n, rec in sorted(parse_attempts(item_dir, "spec").items()):
            if rec.get("status") != "refused":
                continue
            checked += 1
            name = f"spec-{n:02d}"
            stop = gate_stop(rec.get("refusal"))
            cls, provenance = stop["class"], stop["provenance"]
            if cls not in STOP_CLASSES:
                problems.append(f"item {item_dir.name} {name} stop.class {cls!r} names "
                                f"no enumerated class")
                continue
            if provenance not in STOP_PROVENANCES:
                problems.append(f"item {item_dir.name} {name} stop.provenance "
                                f"{provenance!r} names no enumerated provenance")
                continue
            if not stop.get("detail"):
                problems.append(f"item {item_dir.name} {name} is a gate stop with no cause")

    older_note = f"{older} record(s) before stop was recorded"
    if problems:
        return p.fail("; ".join(problems), older_records=older)
    return p.hold(f"{checked} checked ({older_note})" if checked == 0
                 else f"{checked} non-pass record(s) carry stop with a cause ({older_note})",
                 older_records=older)


def attestation_command(m: dict) -> str | None:
    """[forge].attestation_command: the tenant's own read of the store,
    given a merge sha on stdin, printing the statement as JSON on exit 0
    and nothing on a non-zero exit. Absent is not passing (issue #158:
    the doctor said done while #106, #148, #153 had no statement in the
    store at all -- there was no key to read one through)."""
    cmd = (m.get("forge") or {}).get("attestation_command")
    return cmd if isinstance(cmd, str) and cmd.strip() else None


def statement_for(root: Path, cmd: str, merge_sha: str) -> dict | None:
    """The statement the forge holds for merge_sha, or None when the
    command exits non-zero or prints anything but a JSON object."""
    run = subprocess.run(cmd, shell=True, cwd=str(root), input=merge_sha + "\n",
                         capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if run.returncode != 0:
        return None
    try:
        parsed = json.loads(run.stdout)
    except ValueError:
        return None
    return parsed if isinstance(parsed, dict) else None


def _is_ancestor(root: Path, earlier: str, later: str) -> bool:
    run = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(root), "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", earlier, later],
                         capture_output=True, check=False)
    return run.returncode == 0


def proof_every_attested_item_is_sealed(root: Path, m: dict | None) -> Proof:
    p = Proof("every-attested-item-is-sealed", "every attested item has its statement in the store")
    if m is None:
        return p.fail(f"no {MANIFEST} at {root}; no records directory is declared")
    cmd = attestation_command(m)
    if cmd is None:
        return p.fail("no [forge].attestation_command in the manifest; the store cannot be read")
    records_rel = m.get("paths", {}).get("records", "")
    records_dir = root / records_rel
    if not records_dir.is_dir():
        return p.fail(f"no records directory under {records_dir}")
    items = load_records(records_dir)
    complete = [item for item, stages in sorted(items.items()) if all(s in stages for s in STAGES)]

    # [forge].sealed_since: merges at or before this sha predate the
    # tenant's merge queue -- the self-tenant's own #106, #148, #153 were
    # merged direct, with no merge_group run to seal them. They are
    # named, never failed, the same window convention proof three keeps
    # for commits before the manifest's own install.
    since = (m.get("forge") or {}).get("sealed_since")
    merges, refusal = _merges_reachable(root)
    if refusal:
        return p.fail(refusal)
    sealed: list[str] = []
    before_window: list[str] = []
    unsealed: list[str] = []
    not_merged: list[str] = []
    reasons: list[str] = []
    for item in complete:
        merge_sha, _ = merge_introducing_records(root, records_rel, item, STAGES, merges=merges)
        if merge_sha is None:
            not_merged.append(item)
            continue
        if isinstance(since, str) and since.strip() and _is_ancestor(root, merge_sha, since):
            before_window.append(item)
        elif statement_for(root, cmd, merge_sha) is not None:
            sealed.append(item)
        else:
            unsealed.append(item)
            reasons.append(f"item {item} merged at {merge_sha[:12]} has no statement in the store")
    if reasons:
        return p.fail("; ".join(reasons), sealed=sealed, before_window=before_window,
                      unsealed=unsealed, not_merged=not_merged)
    return p.hold(f"{len(sealed)} merged item(s) sealed, {len(before_window)} before the window, "
                 f"{len(not_merged)} not merged",
                 sealed=sealed, before_window=before_window, unsealed=unsealed, not_merged=not_merged)


def doctor(root: Path) -> list[Proof]:
    m = read_manifest(root)
    return [proof_every_gate_rejects(root, m),
            proof_one_item_every_stage(root, m),
            proof_no_commit_lacks_record(root, m),
            proof_no_stop_lacks_cause(root, m),
            proof_no_invocation_lacks_record(root, m),
            proof_every_attested_item_is_sealed(root, m)]


def cmd_doctor(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    as_json = "--json" in argv
    args = [a for a in argv if a != "--json"]
    root = Path(args[0] if args else ".").resolve()
    proofs = doctor(root)
    if as_json:
        print(json.dumps({"root": str(root), "done": all(p.holds for p in proofs),
                          "proofs": [p.as_dict() for p in proofs]}, indent=2))
    else:
        for i, p in enumerate(proofs, 1):
            print(f"proof {i} {p.key}: {'HOLDS' if p.holds else 'FAILS'}: {p.reason}")
        print("doctor: done" if all(p.holds for p in proofs)
              else f"doctor: not done ({sum(not p.holds for p in proofs)} of "
                   f"{len(proofs)} proofs fail)")
    return 0 if all(p.holds for p in proofs) else 1


# --- the spec contract ---

def parse_spec(spec_md: Path) -> tuple[dict | None, str | None]:
    lines = spec_md.read_text().splitlines()
    heading_idx = [i for i, l in enumerate(lines) if l.startswith("## ")]
    headings = [lines[i].rstrip() for i in heading_idx]
    expected = ["## Problem", "## Claims", "## Criteria"]
    if headings != expected:
        bad = next((h for h in headings if h not in expected), None)
        if bad is None:
            missing = [h for h in expected if h not in headings]
            bad = missing[0] if missing else "heading order"
        return None, f"headings: found {headings or ['(none)']}, expected {expected} ({bad})"
    idx_problem, idx_claims, idx_criteria = heading_idx
    claims = [l.strip()[2:].strip() for l in lines[idx_claims + 1:idx_criteria]
              if l.strip().startswith("- ")]
    criteria = []
    for l in lines[idx_criteria + 1:]:
        s = l.strip()
        if not s.startswith("- ") or ":" not in s:
            continue
        name = s[2:].split(":", 1)[0].strip()
        criteria.append(name)
    return {"claims": claims, "criteria": criteria}, None


def run_criterion(exe: Path, cwd: Path) -> int:
    try:
        run = subprocess.run([str(exe)], cwd=str(cwd), capture_output=True,
                             timeout=CRITERION_TIMEOUT, check=False)
        return run.returncode
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return 124
    except OSError:
        return 126


def parse_removed(stub_dir: Path) -> list[str]:
    """The paths listed in stub/.removed, one per line, comments (lines
    starting with #) and blank lines ignored. [] when the stub carries no
    .removed at all.
    """
    removed_file = stub_dir / ".removed"
    if not removed_file.is_file():
        return []
    paths = []
    for line in removed_file.read_text().splitlines():
        s = line.strip()
        if not s or s.startswith("#"):
            continue
        paths.append(s)
    return paths


def removed_path_refusal(root: Path, stub_dir: Path, rel: str) -> str | None:
    p = Path(rel)
    if p.is_absolute() or ".." in p.parts:
        return f"stub/.removed names a path outside the tree: {rel}"
    if not (root / p).exists():
        return f"stub/.removed names a path not in the tree: {rel}"
    if (stub_dir / p).exists():
        return f"stub/.removed names a path the stub also supplies: {rel}"
    return None


def stub_removed_refusal(root: Path, spec_dir: Path) -> str | None:
    """Validated by name, before any criterion runs -- a stub whose
    .removed cannot be applied has no meaning to gate against.
    """
    stub_dir = spec_dir / "stub"
    for rel in parse_removed(stub_dir):
        refusal = removed_path_refusal(root, stub_dir, rel)
        if refusal:
            return refusal
    return None


def run_criteria(root: Path, spec_dir: Path, names: list[str]) -> list[dict]:
    criteria_dir = spec_dir / "criteria"
    stub_dir = spec_dir / "stub"
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        tree = Path(tmp) / "tree"
        stubbed = Path(tmp) / "stubbed"
        shutil.copytree(root, tree, ignore=shutil.ignore_patterns(".git"))
        shutil.copytree(root, stubbed, ignore=shutil.ignore_patterns(".git"))
        if stub_dir.is_dir():
            shutil.copytree(stub_dir, stubbed, dirs_exist_ok=True)
            for rel in parse_removed(stub_dir):
                target = stubbed / rel
                if target.is_dir():
                    shutil.rmtree(target)
                elif target.exists():
                    target.unlink()
            removed_marker = stubbed / ".removed"
            if removed_marker.exists():
                removed_marker.unlink()
        results = []
        for name in names:
            exe = criteria_dir / name
            results.append({"name": name,
                            "on_tree": run_criterion(exe, tree),
                            "on_stub": run_criterion(exe, stubbed)})
        return results


def commit_postdates(root: Path, earlier: str, later: str) -> bool:
    """Whether later is a strict descendant of earlier on this branch."""
    if earlier == later:
        return False
    run = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(root), "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", earlier, later],
                         capture_output=True, check=False)
    return run.returncode == 0


def cmd_spec_gate(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    root = Path(".").resolve()
    item_id = argv[0] if argv else derive_item_id(root)
    if not item_id:
        print("attavo spec-gate: cannot derive item id; pass N or run on an attavo/N-* branch",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    m = read_manifest(root)
    if m is None:
        print(f"attavo spec-gate: no {MANIFEST} at {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    specs_rel = m["paths"]["specs"]
    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    spec_dir = root / specs_rel / item_id
    spec_md = spec_dir / "spec.md"
    if not spec_md.is_file():
        print(f"attavo spec-gate: no spec at {spec_dir}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    spec_rel = str(Path(specs_rel) / item_id)
    try:
        spec_sha = last_commit_touching(root, spec_rel)
    except ShallowCheckoutError as exc:
        print(f"attavo spec-gate: refused: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    records_dir = root / records_rel / item_id
    try:
        approved = approval_status(root, m, item_id) == "valid"
    except ShallowCheckoutError as exc:
        print(f"attavo spec-gate: refused: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    latest_approval = latest_record(records_dir, "approval")
    if approved:
        record = {"sha": git(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD").strip(), "spec_sha": spec_sha,
                  "at": now_iso(), "status": "approved",
                  "approval_sha": latest_approval.get("sha") if latest_approval else None,
                  "criteria": [], "refusal": None}
        write_record(records_dir, "spec", record, m, item_id)
        print("attavo spec-gate: approved (no criteria run)")
        return 0

    # Third verdict, beside "approved" and Phase 1's "pass": the approval
    # is not valid, but a build already ran after it -- the amendment that
    # expired the approval landed above the line, not below it, and the
    # tree the build produced is what must satisfy the criteria now, not
    # the stub that only ever proved they were reachable before approval.
    # Issue #98: whatever the build's status, including a halt -- the
    # executor commits then asks, so a halted build's sha postdating the
    # approval means it did the work. commit_postdates already excludes a
    # halt that committed nothing, since such a build's sha equals its
    # start_sha and so postdates nothing.
    latest_build = latest_record(records_dir, "build")
    built = bool(
        latest_approval and latest_build
        and commit_postdates(root, latest_approval["sha"], latest_build["sha"]))

    parsed, refusal = parse_spec(spec_md)
    results: list[dict] = []
    if refusal is None:
        if built:
            refusal = stub_removed_refusal(root, spec_dir)
            if refusal is None:
                # Per criterion, not the set: passing on the tree is what
                # the build already satisfied; failing on the tree but
                # passing on the stub is a new obligation the next build
                # must meet -- an operator scripting a ruling the executor
                # skipped. Refused only when a criterion satisfies neither.
                results = run_criteria(root, spec_dir, parsed["criteria"])
                fail = next((c for c in results if c["on_tree"] != 0 and c["on_stub"] != 0), None)
                if fail:
                    refusal = f"criterion fails against the tree plus stub: {fail['name']}"
        elif not parsed["claims"]:
            refusal = "claims: empty; the build may touch nothing"
        else:
            criteria_dir = spec_dir / "criteria"
            listed = parsed["criteria"]
            existing = sorted(p.name for p in criteria_dir.iterdir()) if criteria_dir.is_dir() else []
            prose = None
            for name in listed:
                if name not in existing:
                    prose = name
                    break
            if prose is None:
                for name in existing:
                    if name not in listed:
                        prose = name
                        break
            if prose is None:
                for name in listed:
                    if not os.access(criteria_dir / name, os.X_OK):
                        prose = name
                        break
            if prose:
                refusal = f"criterion is prose: {prose}"
            else:
                refusal = stub_removed_refusal(root, spec_dir)
                if refusal is None:
                    results = run_criteria(root, spec_dir, listed)
                    fail_tree = next((c for c in results if c["on_tree"] == 0), None)
                    if fail_tree:
                        refusal = f"criterion passes against the tree: {fail_tree['name']}"
                    else:
                        fail_stub = next((c for c in results if c["on_stub"] != 0), None)
                        if fail_stub:
                            refusal = f"criterion fails against the tree plus stub: {fail_stub['name']}"

    status = "refused" if refusal else ("built" if built else "pass")
    record = {"sha": git(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD").strip(),
              "spec_sha": spec_sha,
              "at": now_iso(), "status": status, "approval_sha": None,
              "criteria": results, "refusal": refusal}
    write_record(records_dir, "spec", record, m, item_id)

    if refusal:
        print(f"attavo spec-gate: refused: {refusal}")
        return 1
    print(f"attavo spec-gate: {status} ({len(results)} criterion/criteria)")
    return 0


# --- approval, bound to a commit ---

def cmd_approve(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    root = Path(".").resolve()
    by = None
    positional: list[str] = []
    i = 0
    while i < len(argv):
        if argv[i] == "--by":
            by = argv[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(argv) else None
            i += 2
        else:
            positional.append(argv[i])
            i += 1
    item_id = positional[0] if positional else derive_item_id(root)
    if not item_id:
        print("attavo approve: cannot derive item id; pass N or run on an attavo/N-* branch",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if not by:
        print("attavo approve: --by LOGIN is required", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    m = read_manifest(root)
    if m is None:
        print(f"attavo approve: no {MANIFEST} at {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    specs_rel = m["paths"]["specs"]
    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    spec_rel = str(Path(specs_rel) / item_id)
    try:
        spec_sha_now = last_commit_touching(root, spec_rel)
    except ShallowCheckoutError as exc:
        print(f"attavo approve: refused: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    records_dir = root / records_rel / item_id
    latest_spec = latest_record(records_dir, "spec")
    approvers = ((m.get("operators") or {}).get("approvers")) or []

    refusal = None
    if latest_spec is None or latest_spec.get("status") not in ("pass", "built"):
        refusal = f"no passing spec-gate record for item {item_id}"
    elif latest_spec.get("spec_sha") != spec_sha_now:
        refusal = (f"spec-gate record is stale: recorded {latest_spec.get('spec_sha')}, "
                   f"spec now at {spec_sha_now}")
    elif by not in approvers:
        refusal = f"{by} is not in [operators].approvers"

    if refusal:
        print(f"attavo approve: refused: {refusal}")
        return 1

    record = {"sha": git(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD").strip(), "spec_sha": spec_sha_now,
              "approved_by": by, "at": now_iso()}
    write_record(records_dir, "approval", record, m, item_id)
    print(f"attavo approve: item {item_id} approved by {by}")
    return 0


def approval_status(root: Path, m: dict, item_id: str) -> str:
    """"absent", "expired", or "valid": whether the latest approval record
    for item_id still binds to the spec's current commit. Raises
    ShallowCheckoutError on a shallow checkout, same as last_commit_touching.
    Shared by cmd_approval_status and cmd_build so build refuses on exactly
    what `attavo approval-status` would print.
    """
    specs_rel = m["paths"]["specs"]
    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    spec_rel = str(Path(specs_rel) / item_id)
    spec_sha_now = last_commit_touching(root, spec_rel)
    records_dir = root / records_rel / item_id
    latest_approval = latest_record(records_dir, "approval")

    if latest_approval is None:
        return "absent"
    if latest_approval.get("spec_sha") == spec_sha_now:
        return "valid"
    return "expired"


def cmd_approval_status(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    root = Path(".").resolve()
    item_id = argv[0] if argv else derive_item_id(root)
    if not item_id:
        print("attavo approval-status: cannot derive item id; pass N or run on an attavo/N-* branch",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    m = read_manifest(root)
    if m is None:
        print(f"attavo approval-status: no {MANIFEST} at {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    try:
        status = approval_status(root, m, item_id)
    except ShallowCheckoutError as exc:
        print(f"attavo approval-status: refused: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    print(status)
    return 0 if status == "valid" else 1


# --- build: adapter, claims boundary, criteria, and the tenant's tests ---

STAGE_NAMES = ("build", "verify", "counsel")


def merge_adapter_config(m: dict, adapter_name: str, stage: str) -> dict:
    """[executor.<adapter>] merged with [executor.<adapter>.<stage>], the
    stage table shadowing the parent for that stage only. Both build and
    verify call this before setting ATTAVO_ADAPTER_CONFIG, and the
    record's adapter_config is the merged result."""
    parent = (m.get("executor") or {}).get(adapter_name) or {}
    if not isinstance(parent, dict):
        return {}
    merged = {k: v for k, v in parent.items() if k not in STAGE_NAMES}
    stage_table = parent.get(stage)
    if isinstance(stage_table, dict):
        merged.update(stage_table)
    return merged


def resolve_adapter(root: Path, name: str) -> Path | None:
    """adapters/<name>/run, resolved first under the tenant's own
    attavo/adapters/, then under this product's adapters/."""
    for base in (root / "attavo" / "adapters", PRODUCT_ROOT / "adapters"):
        candidate = base / name / "run"
        if candidate.is_file():
            return candidate
    return None


def adapter_reports(adapter_path: Path) -> list[str]:
    """Field names adapters/<name>/conformance.json declares this adapter
    can supply with a real value -- vendor independence (2026-09-09
    ruling): the record carries the declaration, so an adapter claiming
    a field and returning null for it fails the conformance suite
    instead of writing a silent, uncomparable null. [] when the
    resolved adapter (a tenant override included) declares no
    conformance.json at all -- an absent declaration, not a refusal."""
    manifest_path = adapter_path.parent / "conformance.json"
    if not manifest_path.is_file():
        return []
    try:
        manifest = json.loads(manifest_path.read_text())
    except json.JSONDecodeError:
        return []
    return manifest.get("reports", [])


def changed_paths(root: Path, start_sha: str, records_rel: str) -> list[str]:
    """Paths changed between start_sha and HEAD, other than paths.records --
    the records directory is where build itself writes, not a claim."""
    out = git(root, "diff", "--name-only", start_sha, "HEAD").splitlines()
    prefix = records_rel.rstrip("/") + "/"
    return [p for p in out if p and p != records_rel and not p.startswith(prefix)]


def matches_a_claim(path: str, claims: list[str]) -> bool:
    return any(fnmatch.fnmatch(path, pattern) for pattern in claims)


def protected_path_touched(root: Path, start_sha: str, records_rel: str,
                           specs_rel: str) -> str | None:
    """The first path an executor's own commits touched under
    paths.records or paths.specs since start_sha, or None (issue #140).
    Neither directory is a claim a spec can make -- paths.records is
    where the product itself writes, paths.specs is the definition of
    done -- yet changed_paths above excludes paths.records from the
    ordinary claims check entirely, so a path there never even reached
    matches_a_claim to be refused: tenant zero's own item #688 committed
    its own events file this way, under its records directory, on a
    build that then passed. Checked directly against the raw diff, not
    changed_paths's already-filtered list."""
    touched = git(root, "diff", "--name-only", start_sha, "HEAD").splitlines()
    protected = []
    for rel in (records_rel, specs_rel):
        stripped = rel.rstrip("/")
        protected.append((stripped, stripped + "/"))
    for p in touched:
        if not p:
            continue
        for exact, prefix in protected:
            if p == exact or p.startswith(prefix):
                return p
    return None


def executor_identity(adapter_name: str) -> dict:
    """The executor's identity (issue #33): set by the product, never the
    tenant's git config, so an executor commit is never indistinguishable
    from an operator's own -- the specimen is attavo/code-factory@e22fa85.
    A reserved, undeliverable domain (.invalid) so no mailbox is implied.
    Distinct from the records app identity: the app says the product
    pushed this, this says an invocation authored this -- different
    facts, neither borrows the other's name."""
    return {"name": f"attavo executor ({adapter_name})",
            "email": f"executor+{adapter_name}@attavo.invalid"}


def driver_identity() -> dict:
    """The driver's identity: set by the product, never the tenant's git
    config, so a driver's own transport commit -- a record commit
    `commit_record` makes on the driver's behalf -- is never
    indistinguishable from an operator's own; `git log --author='attavo
    driver'` on a driven item lists exactly those commits. Distinct from
    executor_identity: that names an adapter invocation's own commits,
    this names the transport that carries a stage's record."""
    return {"name": "attavo driver", "email": "driver@attavo.invalid"}


# The stop classes issue #89 gave no code of their own beyond the guard
# that raised them -- non-question and outlived-invocation from an
# adapter's own pre-emptive refusal, forge-unconcluded from a returned
# verify, ceiling from a declared-input-limit error, claims-boundary from
# the write-outside-claims refusal, repeated-act from... the same shape,
# environment from cmd_build's own [checks].command preflight (issue
# #115). question (a person's to answer) and the residual (no-cause,
# unclassed -- nobody has looked yet) are not in this tuple; attavo stops
# (issue #129) is the one reader, so it lives beside driver_identity,
# not beside derive_stop, which never needs to tell a guard from any
# other class once it has already named one.
GUARDED_STOP_CLASSES = ("non-question", "outlived-invocation", "forge-unconcluded", "ceiling",
                        "claims-boundary", "repeated-act", "environment")


def stop_class_enum() -> tuple[str, ...]:
    """Every class a stop can name, read from the build record schema's own
    enum (issue #129) rather than retyped -- STOP_CLASSES already drifted
    once (environment arrived in the schema before anything but derive_stop
    read it); a second copy is a second place to forget. build's schema is
    read, not verify's: verify's own enum is a strict subset (it never
    raises environment, cmd_build's own preflight alone)."""
    schema = stage_schema("build")
    return tuple(schema["properties"]["stop"]["properties"]["class"]["enum"])


def commits_since(root: Path, start_sha: str, end: str = "HEAD") -> list[dict]:
    """(sha, name, email) for every commit after start_sha up to and
    including end, first-parent only, oldest first. cmd_build's own call
    (start_sha at the invocation, end left at the default HEAD) never sees
    a merge in that window, so --first-parent changes nothing there;
    issue #128's ledger is the caller that needs it, to walk one branch's
    own line without a concurrent merge to main expanding into every
    commit main gained meanwhile."""
    out = git(root, "log", "--first-parent", "--reverse",
             "--format=%H\x1f%an\x1f%ae", f"{start_sha}..{end}")
    commits = []
    for line in out.splitlines():
        if not line.strip():
            continue
        sha, name, email = line.split("\x1f")
        commits.append({"sha": sha, "name": name, "email": email})
    return commits


def commits_with_diffs(root: Path, start_sha: str, end: str = "HEAD") -> list[dict]:
    """commits_since's own (sha, name, email), each with its own
    first-parent diff already attached as "touched" -- one process for the
    whole range rather than commits_since's list plus a git diff per
    commit (issue #153, the cost item_ledger's own walk paid per commit)."""
    out = git(root, "log", "--first-parent", "--reverse", "--diff-merges=first-parent",
             "--name-only", "--format=\x01%H\x1f%an\x1f%ae", f"{start_sha}..{end}")
    commits = []
    for block in out.split("\x01")[1:]:
        header, _, rest = block.partition("\n")
        sha, name, email = header.split("\x1f")
        commits.append({"sha": sha, "name": name, "email": email,
                        "touched": [line for line in rest.splitlines() if line.strip()]})
    return commits


def split_adapter_stdout(stdout: str) -> tuple[str, dict | None]:
    """(transcript, payload): the adapter's last non-empty stdout line is
    its JSON report, and everything before it is transcript, only when
    that last line actually parses as JSON. When it does not -- a tool's
    own error line printed last on a non-zero exit (issue #86: tenant
    zero attest/records/659/verify-01.log, codex's own "Input exceeds..."
    line, dropped because it was mistaken for an attempted payload) --
    nothing here is a report, and the whole stdout is the transcript, so
    that last line is not silently lost.
    """
    lines = stdout.splitlines()
    non_empty = [i for i, l in enumerate(lines) if l.strip()]
    if not non_empty:
        return stdout, None
    last = non_empty[-1]
    try:
        payload = json.loads(lines[last])
    except ValueError:
        return stdout, None
    transcript = "\n".join(lines[:last])
    return transcript, payload


# --- events: the adapter contract's one optional output (issue #26) --
# an uncommitted <stage>-NN.events.jsonl the screen tails while a stage
# runs, never a record and never committed (commit_record names only
# .json, .log, and .filed; this suffix is never among them). Written
# under the checkout's own git directory, not the working tree (issue
# #140): tenant zero's own item #688 committed its events file for
# real -- the executor's own commit staged it, because it sat inside
# the tree the executor runs `git status` against. ---

EVENT_TEXT_MAX_CHARS = 400


def stage_events_dir(root: Path) -> Path:
    """<git-dir>/attavo, absolute -- <checkout>/.git/attavo for an
    ordinary checkout, <main-repo>/.git/worktrees/<name>/attavo for a
    linked worktree (issue #140). Outside the working tree either way,
    so a file written here never appears in `git status` in root and is
    never at risk of being staged and committed by the executor; the
    screen's seam finds it the same way, with the same command, in its
    own worktree."""
    git_dir = Path(git(root, "rev-parse", "--absolute-git-dir").strip())
    events_dir = git_dir / "attavo"
    events_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    return events_dir


def append_event(events_path: Path, kind: str, text: str) -> None:
    """One {at, kind, text} JSON line appended to events_path and flushed
    at once (open-write-close, not a kept handle) -- the screen tails
    this file while the stage is still running, so a line held in a
    buffer is a line the feed does not move on. text is collapsed to one
    line and capped at EVENT_TEXT_MAX_CHARS: a tool result can be a whole
    file, and the feed renders lines, not files."""
    line = {"at": now_iso(), "kind": kind,
            "text": " ".join(text.split())[:EVENT_TEXT_MAX_CHARS]}
    with events_path.open("a") as fh:
        fh.write(json.dumps(line) + "\n")


def cmd_build(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    root = Path(".").resolve()
    item_id = argv[0] if argv else derive_item_id(root)
    if not item_id:
        print("attavo build: cannot derive item id; pass N or run on an attavo/N-* branch",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    m = read_manifest(root)
    if m is None:
        print(f"attavo build: no {MANIFEST} at {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    adapter_name = (m.get("executor") or {}).get("build")
    if not adapter_name:
        print("attavo build: refused: no [executor].build in the manifest")
        return 1

    tests_cfg = m.get("tests") or {}
    if not tests_cfg.get("select") or not tests_cfg.get("run"):
        print("attavo build: refused: no [tests] table in the manifest "
              "([tests].select and [tests].run are both required)")
        return 1

    try:
        status = approval_status(root, m, item_id)
    except ShallowCheckoutError as exc:
        print(f"attavo build: refused: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if status != "valid":
        print(f"attavo build: refused: approval is {status}")
        return 1

    adapter_path = resolve_adapter(root, adapter_name)
    if adapter_path is None:
        print(f"attavo build: refused: no adapter named {adapter_name!r}")
        return 1

    specs_rel = m["paths"]["specs"]
    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    spec_dir = root / specs_rel / item_id
    item_records_dir = root / records_rel / item_id
    spec_sha = last_commit_touching(root, str(Path(specs_rel) / item_id))
    latest_approval = latest_record(item_records_dir, "approval")
    parsed, _ = parse_spec(spec_dir / "spec.md")
    claims = parsed["claims"] if parsed else []
    criteria_names = parsed["criteria"] if parsed else []
    adapter_config = merge_adapter_config(m, adapter_name, "build")
    checks_command = (m.get("checks") or {}).get("command")
    identity = executor_identity(adapter_name)
    reports = adapter_reports(adapter_path)

    start_sha = git(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD").strip()
    branch = git(root, "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD").strip()
    started = time.monotonic()

    # Issue #115: the tenant's whole-tree checks run once here, on the
    # approved tree, before the adapter is ever invoked -- exit 2 means
    # the command itself cannot run (e.g. a missing import at test
    # collection, item #106's build-01), not a failing check, and no
    # build could have passed against it. Exit 0 or 1 -- the checks run
    # and pass or fail, the expected states before a build -- proceeds;
    # the per-iteration checks below run (and are recorded) exactly as
    # before.
    if checks_command:
        preflight = subprocess.run(checks_command, shell=True, cwd=str(root),
                                   capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
        if preflight.returncode == 2:
            detail = _log_last_line(preflight.stdout + "\n" + preflight.stderr)
            record = {
                "sha": start_sha,
                "start_sha": start_sha,
                "spec_sha": spec_sha,
                "approval_sha": latest_approval.get("sha") if latest_approval else None,
                "at": now_iso(),
                "adapter": adapter_name,
                "adapter_config": adapter_config,
                "executor_identity": identity,
                "reports": reports,
                "adapter_version": None,
                "model": None,
                "model_version": None,
                "iterations": 1,
                "criteria": [],
                "selected_tests": None,
                "checks": {"command": checks_command, "exit": preflight.returncode},
                "tokens": {"input": 0, "output": 0},
                "cost_usd": None,
                "wall_clock_s": round(time.monotonic() - started, 3),
                "status": "refused",
                "refusal": "[checks].command exited 2 on the approved tree",
                "halted": None,
                "stop": {"class": "environment", "provenance": "tenant", "detail": detail},
            }
            record_path = write_record(item_records_dir, "build", record, m, item_id,
                                       log=preflight.stdout + preflight.stderr)
            print(f"attavo build: refused ({record_path.name})")
            return 1

    adapter_version = model = model_version = None
    tokens = {"input": 0, "output": 0}
    cost_usd = None
    transcripts: list[str] = []
    criteria_results: list[dict] = []
    selected_tests: dict | None = None
    checks_result: dict | None = None
    status_out = "failed"
    refusal = None
    refusal_provenance = None
    halted = None
    iterations_used = 0

    events_path = stage_events_dir(root) / f"build-{next_attempt_number(item_records_dir, 'build'):02d}.events.jsonl"

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        feedback_path = Path(tmp) / "feedback.txt"
        feedback_path.write_text("")

        for iteration in range(1, BUILD_ITERATION_BUDGET + 1):
            iterations_used = iteration
            append_event(events_path, "iteration", f"iteration {iteration} of {BUILD_ITERATION_BUDGET}")
            env = dict(os.environ)
            env["ATTAVO_SPEC_DIR"] = str(spec_dir)
            env["ATTAVO_RECORDS_DIR"] = str(item_records_dir)
            env["ATTAVO_RULINGS"] = str(root / m["paths"]["rulings"])
            env["ATTAVO_BRANCH"] = branch
            env["ATTAVO_ITERATION"] = str(iteration)
            env["ATTAVO_FEEDBACK"] = str(feedback_path)
            env["ATTAVO_ADAPTER_CONFIG"] = json.dumps(adapter_config)
            env["ATTAVO_STAGE"] = "build"
            env["ATTAVO_EVENTS"] = str(events_path)
            if checks_command:
                env["ATTAVO_CHECKS_COMMAND"] = checks_command
            env["GIT_AUTHOR_NAME"] = identity["name"]
            env["GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL"] = identity["email"]
            env["GIT_COMMITTER_NAME"] = identity["name"]
            env["GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL"] = identity["email"]

            run = subprocess.run([str(adapter_path)], cwd=str(root), env=env,
                                 capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
            transcript, payload = split_adapter_stdout(run.stdout)
            if run.returncode != 0:
                # The guarantee behind issue #53's refusal: an adapter that
                # exits non-zero always leaves a non-empty log, even one
                # that printed nothing at all on stderr.
                detail = f"[adapter exited {run.returncode}]"
                if run.stderr.strip():
                    detail += "\n" + run.stderr.rstrip("\n")
                transcript = (transcript + "\n" if transcript else "") + detail
            transcripts.append(transcript)

            if run.returncode != 0 or payload is None or not payload.get("model"):
                status_out = "failed"
                break

            adapter_version = payload.get("adapter_version")
            model = payload.get("model")
            model_version = payload.get("model_version")
            tok = payload.get("tokens") or {}
            tokens["input"] += tok.get("input", 0) or 0
            tokens["output"] += tok.get("output", 0) or 0
            iter_cost = payload.get("cost_usd")
            if iter_cost is not None:
                cost_usd = (cost_usd or 0) + iter_cost

            if payload.get("status") == "question":
                status_out = "halted"
                halted = {"question": payload.get("question")}
                break

            foreign = next((c for c in commits_since(root, start_sha)
                           if c["name"] != identity["name"] or c["email"] != identity["email"]),
                          None)
            if foreign:
                status_out = "refused"
                refusal = f"commit {foreign['sha']} not authored by the executor"
                break

            protected = protected_path_touched(root, start_sha, records_rel, specs_rel)
            if protected:
                status_out = "refused"
                refusal = protected
                refusal_provenance = "executor"
                break

            touched = sorted(changed_paths(root, start_sha, records_rel))
            bad_path = next((p for p in touched if not matches_a_claim(p, claims)), None)
            if bad_path:
                status_out = "refused"
                refusal = bad_path
                break

            criteria_results = [
                {"name": name, "exit": run_criterion(spec_dir / "criteria" / name, root)}
                for name in criteria_names]
            for c in criteria_results:
                append_event(events_path, "gate", f"{c['name']}: exit {c['exit']}")

            select_in = "".join(p + "\n" for p in touched)
            select_run = subprocess.run(tests_cfg["select"], shell=True, cwd=str(root),
                                        input=select_in, capture_output=True,
                                        text=True, check=False)
            identifiers = [l for l in select_run.stdout.splitlines() if l.strip()]
            run_in = "".join(i + "\n" for i in identifiers)
            test_run = subprocess.run(tests_cfg["run"], shell=True, cwd=str(root),
                                      input=run_in, capture_output=True,
                                      text=True, check=False)
            selected_tests = {"select": tests_cfg["select"], "run": tests_cfg["run"],
                              "count": len(identifiers), "exit": test_run.returncode}

            checks_run = None
            if checks_command:
                checks_run = subprocess.run(checks_command, shell=True, cwd=str(root),
                                            capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
                checks_result = {"command": checks_command, "exit": checks_run.returncode}

            # Issue #89, rejection test 1: the gate output that decides pass
            # or fail is appended to the build log itself, under a heading
            # per iteration, before this temporary directory goes away --
            # tenant zero's #576 build-11 and #659 build-01 are a failed
            # record with nothing beside it to say what was red, because
            # this output previously lived only in feedback.txt, in a
            # tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() cmd_build deletes on return.
            gate_lines = [f"criterion {c['name']}: exit {c['exit']}" for c in criteria_results]
            gate_lines.append(f"selected tests ({selected_tests['count']}): exit "
                              f"{selected_tests['exit']}")
            if test_run.stdout.strip():
                gate_lines += ["--- selected tests stdout ---", test_run.stdout.rstrip("\n")]
            if test_run.stderr.strip():
                gate_lines += ["--- selected tests stderr ---", test_run.stderr.rstrip("\n")]
            if checks_run is not None:
                tail = (checks_run.stdout.splitlines() + checks_run.stderr.splitlines())
                tail = tail[-CHECKS_FEEDBACK_TAIL_LINES:]
                gate_lines.append(f"tenant checks: exit {checks_run.returncode}")
                gate_lines += ["--- checks output (tail) ---", *tail]
            transcripts[-1] += (f"\n--- attavo gates, iteration {iteration} ---\n"
                               + "\n".join(gate_lines))

            failing = [c for c in criteria_results if c["exit"] != 0]
            checks_ok = checks_run is None or checks_run.returncode == 0
            if not failing and test_run.returncode == 0 and checks_ok:
                status_out = "pass"
                break

            if iteration == BUILD_ITERATION_BUDGET:
                status_out = "failed"
                break

            feedback_lines = []
            if failing:
                feedback_lines.append("criteria failing:")
                feedback_lines += [f"- {c['name']}: exit {c['exit']}" for c in failing]
            if test_run.returncode != 0:
                feedback_lines += [f"test run exit {test_run.returncode}",
                                   "--- stdout ---", test_run.stdout,
                                   "--- stderr ---", test_run.stderr]
            if checks_run is not None and checks_run.returncode != 0:
                tail = (checks_run.stdout.splitlines() + checks_run.stderr.splitlines())
                tail = tail[-CHECKS_FEEDBACK_TAIL_LINES:]
                feedback_lines += [f"checks exit {checks_run.returncode}",
                                   "--- checks output (tail) ---", *tail]
            feedback_path.write_text("\n".join(feedback_lines) + "\n")

    record = {
        "sha": git(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD").strip(),
        "start_sha": start_sha,
        "spec_sha": spec_sha,
        "approval_sha": latest_approval.get("sha") if latest_approval else None,
        "at": now_iso(),
        "adapter": adapter_name,
        "adapter_config": adapter_config,
        "executor_identity": identity,
        "reports": reports,
        "adapter_version": adapter_version,
        "model": model,
        "model_version": model_version,
        "iterations": iterations_used,
        "criteria": criteria_results,
        "selected_tests": selected_tests,
        "checks": checks_result,
        "tokens": tokens,
        "cost_usd": cost_usd,
        "wall_clock_s": round(time.monotonic() - started, 3),
        "status": status_out,
        "refusal": refusal,
        "halted": halted,
        "stop": derive_stop(status_out, refusal=refusal, halted=halted,
                            log="\n".join(transcripts),
                            report_class=(payload or {}).get("class"),
                            refusal_provenance=refusal_provenance),
    }
    record_path = write_record(item_records_dir, "build", record, m, item_id,
                               log="\n".join(transcripts))

    print(f"attavo build: {status_out} ({record_path.name})")
    if status_out == "halted":
        return BUILD_HALT_EXIT
    return 0 if status_out == "pass" else 1


# --- verify: the forge's own read first, a judge only once it is green ---

FORGE_OK_CONCLUSIONS = {"success", "neutral", "skipped"}
# issue #84: a concluded check's second token is a conclusion --
# FORGE_OK_CONCLUSIONS, or another concluded one such as failure,
# cancelled, or timed_out; an unconcluded check's is a status instead,
# one of these. drive's own poll (poll_forge_checks) is the one reader
# that needs to tell a still-running check apart from a concluded, not-ok
# one -- read_forge_checks itself only ever needed "green or not".
FORGE_UNCONCLUDED_STATUSES = {"queued", "in_progress", "pending", "waiting"}


def read_forge_checks(root: Path, checks_command: str, sha: str) -> dict:
    """Runs the tenant's [forge].checks_command on sha; the product does
    not know what forge that command reads. One line per check as
    "<name> <conclusion>"; a command that fails to run, or any conclusion
    other than success/neutral/skipped, is not green."""
    run = subprocess.run(f"{checks_command} {shlex.quote(sha)}", shell=True, cwd=str(root),
                         capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    conclusions = []
    for line in run.stdout.splitlines():
        line = line.strip()
        if not line:
            continue
        parts = line.rsplit(" ", 1)
        if len(parts) == 2:
            conclusions.append({"name": parts[0], "conclusion": parts[1]})
    # A commit the forge has no record of is not green -- it is unread.
    # The executor's own commit is exactly this: the forge attaches
    # checks to a pull request's head, and that commit stops being the
    # head the moment a record commit lands on top of it. all() over an
    # empty list is True, which is why this is spelled out rather than
    # implied by the loop above.
    ok = run.returncode == 0 and bool(conclusions) and all(
        c["conclusion"] in FORGE_OK_CONCLUSIONS for c in conclusions)
    return {"command": checks_command, "sha": sha, "conclusions": conclusions}, ok


def diff_since_approval(root: Path, approval_sha: str | None) -> tuple[str, str | None]:
    """(diff_text, refusal): the item's own work since approval, not
    everything merged onto the branch since then (issue #52) -- a
    three-dot diff against approval_sha itself is not enough, since
    approval_sha is already an ancestor of HEAD in the normal flow and
    merge-base(approval_sha, HEAD) is just approval_sha again (verified
    empirically, see issue #52). Diffing from the merge-base of HEAD and
    the remote's default branch instead excludes content that arrived by
    merging that branch in, because that content is already present on
    both sides of the merge base. refs/remotes/origin/HEAD is what names
    the default branch without hard-coding "main"; when it is not set,
    there is nothing to diff against and this refuses rather than guess.
    ("", None) when approval_sha is falsy -- nothing to diff from at all.
    Shared by cmd_verify and cmd_counsel, which both hand an adapter this
    same diff."""
    if not approval_sha:
        return "", None
    symref = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "-C", str(root), "symbolic-ref", "-q", "refs/remotes/origin/HEAD"],
        capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if symref.returncode != 0:
        return "", "refs/remotes/origin/HEAD is not set; run `git remote set-head origin -a`"
    diff_base = git(root, "merge-base", symref.stdout.strip(), "HEAD").strip()
    return git(root, "diff", diff_base, "HEAD"), None


def cmd_verify(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    root = Path(".").resolve()
    item_id = argv[0] if argv else derive_item_id(root)
    if not item_id:
        print("attavo verify: cannot derive item id; pass N or run on an attavo/N-* branch",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    m = read_manifest(root)
    if m is None:
        print(f"attavo verify: no {MANIFEST} at {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    specs_rel = m["paths"]["specs"]
    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    item_records_dir = root / records_rel / item_id

    latest_build = latest_record(item_records_dir, "build")
    if latest_build is None:
        print(f"attavo verify: refused: no build record for item {item_id}")
        return 1
    if latest_build.get("status") != "pass":
        print(f"attavo verify: refused: latest build record is "
              f"{latest_build.get('status')!r}, not pass")
        return 1

    forge_cfg = m.get("forge") or {}
    checks_command = forge_cfg.get("checks_command")
    if not checks_command:
        print("attavo verify: refused: no [forge].checks_command in the manifest")
        return 1

    build_sha = latest_build["sha"]
    build_record_path = latest_attempt_path(item_records_dir, "build")
    started = time.monotonic()
    head_sha = git(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD").strip()
    # The forge attaches checks to a pull request's head, not to the
    # executor's own commit -- that commit stops being the head the
    # moment a record commit lands on top of it (issue #44). Reading the
    # forge on head_sha is what the forge actually checked and what will
    # be merged; build_sha and forge_checks["sha"] can differ, and both
    # are kept on the record rather than one sha serving two purposes.
    forge_checks, forge_ok = read_forge_checks(root, checks_command, head_sha)

    if not forge_ok:
        record = {
            "sha": head_sha,
            "build_sha": build_sha,
            "build_record": build_record_path.stem if build_record_path else None,
            "approval_sha": latest_build.get("approval_sha"),
            "at": now_iso(),
            "adapter": None, "adapter_config": {}, "adapter_version": None,
            "model": None, "model_version": None,
            "reports": [],
            "forge_checks": forge_checks,
            "findings": None,
            "tests_named": [],
            "commits": [],
            "mutation": None, "weak_stub": False, "stub_ratio": None,
            "tokens": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "cost_usd": None,
            "wall_clock_s": round(time.monotonic() - started, 3),
            "status": "returned",
            "stop": derive_stop("returned"),
        }
        record_path = write_record(item_records_dir, "verify", record, m, item_id)
        print(f"attavo verify: returned ({record_path.name}); the forge checks are not green")
        return 0

    executor = m.get("executor") or {}
    adapter_name = executor.get("verify") or executor.get("build")
    if not adapter_name:
        print("attavo verify: refused: no [executor].verify or [executor].build in the manifest")
        return 1
    adapter_path = resolve_adapter(root, adapter_name)
    if adapter_path is None:
        print(f"attavo verify: refused: no adapter named {adapter_name!r}")
        return 1

    adapter_config = merge_adapter_config(m, adapter_name, "verify")
    reports = adapter_reports(adapter_path)
    approval_sha = latest_build.get("approval_sha")
    spec_dir = root / specs_rel / item_id
    branch = git(root, "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD").strip()

    diff_text, diff_refusal = diff_since_approval(root, approval_sha)
    if diff_refusal:
        print(f"attavo verify: refused: {diff_refusal}")
        return 1

    events_path = stage_events_dir(root) / f"verify-{next_attempt_number(item_records_dir, 'verify'):02d}.events.jsonl"

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        diff_path = Path(tmp) / "diff.patch"
        diff_path.write_text(diff_text)

        env = dict(os.environ)
        env["ATTAVO_SPEC_DIR"] = str(spec_dir)
        env["ATTAVO_RECORDS_DIR"] = str(item_records_dir)
        env["ATTAVO_RULINGS"] = str(root / m["paths"]["rulings"])
        env["ATTAVO_BRANCH"] = branch
        env["ATTAVO_STAGE"] = "verify"
        env["ATTAVO_DIFF"] = str(diff_path)
        env["ATTAVO_BUILD_RECORD"] = str(build_record_path) if build_record_path else ""
        env["ATTAVO_ADAPTER_CONFIG"] = json.dumps(adapter_config)
        env["ATTAVO_EVENTS"] = str(events_path)

        run = subprocess.run([str(adapter_path)], cwd=str(root), env=env,
                             capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
        transcript, payload = split_adapter_stdout(run.stdout)
        if run.returncode != 0:
            # Same guarantee as cmd_build's, issue #53: a non-zero adapter
            # exit always leaves a non-empty log.
            detail = f"[adapter exited {run.returncode}]"
            if run.stderr.strip():
                detail += "\n" + run.stderr.rstrip("\n")
            transcript = (transcript + "\n" if transcript else "") + detail

    # Verify is a report, not a commit (issue #46): the adapter is invoked
    # on the branch head it was handed, and must leave that head alone.
    # Checked by comparing the head before the invocation (head_sha,
    # already read above for the forge) against the head after it.
    post_adapter_head = git(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD").strip()
    commits: list[str] = []
    if post_adapter_head != head_sha:
        commits = git(root, "rev-list", f"{head_sha}..{post_adapter_head}", "--reverse").split()

    status_out = "failed"
    findings = None
    tests_named: list[str] = []
    model = model_version = adapter_version_out = None
    tokens = {"input": 0, "output": 0}
    cost_usd = None

    if run.returncode == 0 and payload is not None and payload.get("model"):
        model = payload.get("model")
        model_version = payload.get("model_version")
        adapter_version_out = payload.get("adapter_version")
        tok = payload.get("tokens") or {}
        tokens = {"input": tok.get("input", 0) or 0, "output": tok.get("output", 0) or 0}
        cost_usd = payload.get("cost_usd")
        # "findings" absent (or explicitly null) is not the same as an
        # empty list: the adapter had no channel to report one at all,
        # which is not a clean bill of health (issue #46). A commit
        # during verify fails the attempt regardless of what it reported.
        if not commits and "findings" in payload and payload["findings"] is not None:
            findings = payload["findings"]
            tests_named = payload.get("tests_named") or []
            status_out = "pass"

    record = {
        "sha": head_sha,
        "build_sha": build_sha,
        "build_record": build_record_path.stem if build_record_path else None,
        "approval_sha": approval_sha,
        "at": now_iso(),
        "adapter": adapter_name,
        "adapter_config": adapter_config,
        "adapter_version": adapter_version_out,
        "model": model,
        "model_version": model_version,
        "reports": reports,
        "forge_checks": forge_checks,
        "findings": findings,
        "tests_named": tests_named,
        "commits": commits,
        "mutation": None, "weak_stub": False, "stub_ratio": None,
        "tokens": tokens,
        "cost_usd": cost_usd,
        "wall_clock_s": round(time.monotonic() - started, 3),
        "status": status_out,
        "stop": derive_stop(status_out, log=transcript,
                            report_class=(payload or {}).get("class")),
    }
    record_path = write_record(item_records_dir, "verify", record, m, item_id, log=transcript)

    print(f"attavo verify: {status_out} ({record_path.name})")
    return 0 if status_out == "pass" else 1


# --- counsel: a stop is read inside the product first, before a person
# (issue #100). Read-only -- it commits nothing and writes nothing outside
# paths.records/<N>/ -- and it never acts: it proposes a ruling, a patch,
# or names the one-line question a person must answer. ---

COUNSEL_STAGES = ("build", "verify")


def latest_non_pass_stage_record(records_dir: Path) -> tuple[str | None, dict | None, Path | None]:
    """(stage, record, path) for the item's most recent non-pass build or
    verify record, by its own "at" timestamp -- or, when neither stage
    carries one, the item's latest refused spec-gate record (issue #133):
    a refused gate is the same shape of stop, earlier, and it is read only
    once a build or verify record exists to counsel first, since either
    one already implies the gate that produced it passed. (None, None,
    None) when none of the three exists. "Most recent" rather than
    "build, else verify" so a stop on the stage that ran later (typically
    verify, bound to a build that already passed) is the one counseled."""
    candidates: list[tuple[str, dict, Path]] = []
    for stage in COUNSEL_STAGES:
        path = latest_attempt_path(records_dir, stage)
        if path is None:
            continue
        rec = latest_record(records_dir, stage)
        if rec.get("status") == "pass":
            continue
        candidates.append((stage, rec, path))
    if candidates:
        candidates.sort(key=lambda c: c[1].get("at") or "")
        return candidates[-1]
    spec_path = latest_attempt_path(records_dir, "spec")
    if spec_path is not None:
        spec_rec = latest_record(records_dir, "spec")
        if spec_rec.get("status") == "refused":
            return "spec", spec_rec, spec_path
    return None, None, None


def cmd_counsel(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    root = Path(".").resolve()
    item_id = argv[0] if argv else derive_item_id(root)
    if not item_id:
        print("attavo counsel: cannot derive item id; pass N or run on an attavo/N-* branch",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    m = read_manifest(root)
    if m is None:
        print(f"attavo counsel: no {MANIFEST} at {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    specs_rel = m["paths"]["specs"]
    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    item_records_dir = root / records_rel / item_id

    stage, stop_record, record_path = latest_non_pass_stage_record(item_records_dir)
    if stop_record is None:
        print(f"attavo counsel: refused: no non-pass build, verify, or spec-gate record "
              f"for item {item_id}")
        return 1

    executor = m.get("executor") or {}
    adapter_name = executor.get("counsel") or executor.get("verify")
    if not adapter_name:
        print("attavo counsel: refused: no [executor].counsel or [executor].verify "
              "in the manifest")
        return 1
    adapter_path = resolve_adapter(root, adapter_name)
    if adapter_path is None:
        print(f"attavo counsel: refused: no adapter named {adapter_name!r}")
        return 1

    adapter_config = merge_adapter_config(m, adapter_name, "counsel")
    reports = adapter_reports(adapter_path)
    spec_dir = root / specs_rel / item_id
    log_path = record_path.with_suffix(".log")

    diff_text, diff_refusal = diff_since_approval(root, stop_record.get("approval_sha"))
    if diff_refusal:
        print(f"attavo counsel: refused: {diff_refusal}")
        return 1

    started = time.monotonic()

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        diff_path = Path(tmp) / "diff.patch"
        diff_path.write_text(diff_text)

        env = dict(os.environ)
        env["ATTAVO_SPEC_DIR"] = str(spec_dir)
        env["ATTAVO_RECORDS_DIR"] = str(item_records_dir)
        env["ATTAVO_RULINGS"] = str(root / m["paths"]["rulings"])
        env["ATTAVO_STAGE"] = "counsel"
        env["ATTAVO_COUNSEL_RECORD"] = str(record_path)
        env["ATTAVO_COUNSEL_LOG"] = str(log_path) if log_path.is_file() else ""
        env["ATTAVO_COUNSEL_DIFF"] = str(diff_path)
        env["ATTAVO_MANIFEST"] = str(root / MANIFEST)
        env["ATTAVO_PRODUCT_ROOT"] = str(PRODUCT_ROOT)
        env["ATTAVO_ADAPTER_CONFIG"] = json.dumps(adapter_config)

        run = subprocess.run([str(adapter_path)], cwd=str(root), env=env,
                             capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
        transcript, payload = split_adapter_stdout(run.stdout)
        if run.returncode != 0:
            detail = f"[adapter exited {run.returncode}]"
            if run.stderr.strip():
                detail += "\n" + run.stderr.rstrip("\n")
            transcript = (transcript + "\n" if transcript else "") + detail

    status_out = "failed"
    model = model_version = adapter_version_out = None
    tokens = {"input": 0, "output": 0}
    cost_usd = None
    cls = verdict = None
    citations: list = []
    proposal = {"ruling_text": None, "patch": None, "question": None}

    if (run.returncode == 0 and payload is not None
            and payload.get("verdict") in ("answer", "patch", "person")):
        status_out = "done"
        model = payload.get("model")
        model_version = payload.get("model_version")
        adapter_version_out = payload.get("adapter_version")
        tok = payload.get("tokens") or {}
        tokens = {"input": tok.get("input", 0) or 0, "output": tok.get("output", 0) or 0}
        cost_usd = payload.get("cost_usd")
        cls = payload.get("class")
        verdict = payload.get("verdict")
        citations = payload.get("citations") or []
        proposal = payload.get("proposal") or proposal

    record = {
        "sha": git(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD").strip(),
        "record_stage": stage,
        "record": record_path.stem,
        "at": now_iso(),
        "adapter": adapter_name,
        "adapter_config": adapter_config,
        "adapter_version": adapter_version_out,
        "model": model,
        "model_version": model_version,
        "reports": reports,
        "tokens": tokens,
        "cost_usd": cost_usd,
        "wall_clock_s": round(time.monotonic() - started, 3),
        "status": status_out,
        "class": cls,
        "verdict": verdict,
        "citations": citations,
        "proposal": proposal,
    }
    record_out_path = write_record(item_records_dir, "counsel", record, m, item_id, log=transcript)

    print(f"attavo counsel: {status_out} ({record_out_path.name})")
    return 0 if status_out == "done" else 1


# --- drive: the mechanical transitions between approval and ready-to-merge
# (issue #84) -- one step per invocation, and only that step, until a
# state that belongs to a person. Writes no record of its own; the stage
# records already on disk are the only state it reads. ---

def forge_checks_unconcluded(forge_checks: dict) -> bool:
    """Whether poll_forge_checks should keep waiting rather than stop at
    once: an empty read (the forge has not attached any check to this sha
    yet) or any one check still carrying a status (queued, in_progress,
    pending, waiting) rather than a concluded conclusion. A concluded
    conclusion that is not green (failure, cancelled, timed_out, and
    anything else read_forge_checks did not recognize as ok) is not this
    -- that check is done, whether or not it passed."""
    conclusions = forge_checks["conclusions"]
    if not conclusions:
        return True
    return any(c["conclusion"] in FORGE_UNCONCLUDED_STATUSES for c in conclusions)


def poll_forge_checks(root: Path, checks_command: str, sha: str) -> tuple[dict, bool]:
    """read_forge_checks, retried within drive_forge_poll_bound_s() while
    some check is still unconcluded (forge_checks_unconcluded) -- a check
    still running is not yet red, and drive gives it a little room to
    conclude before it gives up. A read where every check has already
    concluded, green or not, stops here at once: there is nothing left to
    wait on. The bound and interval are constants this lane names, not a
    manifest key, re-read at call time so a test's own clock
    (ATTAVO_DRIVE_POLL_BOUND_S, ATTAVO_DRIVE_POLL_INTERVAL_S) never has to
    survive past when it was set."""
    bound = drive_forge_poll_bound_s()
    interval = drive_forge_poll_interval_s()
    deadline = time.monotonic() + bound
    forge_checks, ok = read_forge_checks(root, checks_command, sha)
    while not ok and forge_checks_unconcluded(forge_checks) and time.monotonic() < deadline:
        time.sleep(interval)
        forge_checks, ok = read_forge_checks(root, checks_command, sha)
    return forge_checks, ok


def commit_record(root: Path, record_paths: Path | list[Path], message: str) -> None:
    """`git commit --only` on every record path given, and each one's .log
    and .filed, each if one exists -- never the whole index. A transport
    commit that sweeps the index is exactly the regression issue #46
    already guards build and verify against on the adapter side; drive
    must not reintroduce it on the transport side. The .filed marker
    (issue #89) is committed alongside the record it names, in the same
    commit and the same push, so a second worktree or a fresh clone reads
    the same filed fact rather than refiling every stop it finds with no
    marker of its own. A single Path is accepted for the common one-record
    case; issue #100 passes a list so the counsel record lands in the same
    commit as the stage record it counsels. Authored and committed as
    driver_identity(), set by the product and never the tenant's git
    config, so a driven item's record commits are never indistinguishable
    from an operator's own."""
    if isinstance(record_paths, Path):
        record_paths = [record_paths]
    paths: list[str] = []
    for record_path in record_paths:
        paths.append(str(record_path.relative_to(root)))
        log_path = record_path.with_suffix(".log")
        if log_path.is_file():
            paths.append(str(log_path.relative_to(root)))
        marker_path = record_path.with_suffix(".filed")
        if marker_path.is_file():
            paths.append(str(marker_path.relative_to(root)))
    identity = driver_identity()
    env = dict(os.environ)
    env["GIT_AUTHOR_NAME"] = identity["name"]
    env["GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL"] = identity["email"]
    env["GIT_COMMITTER_NAME"] = identity["name"]
    env["GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL"] = identity["email"]
    git(root, "add", *paths)
    git(root, "commit", "--only", "-m", message, *paths, env=env)


def push_record_commit(root: Path, branch: str) -> tuple[bool, str | None]:
    """Push HEAD to branch on origin. A push a divergent remote rejects is
    retried once after `git pull --no-rebase --no-edit origin branch` -- a
    merge, never a rebase, because a rebase would change the sha the record
    commit just wrote already names."""
    def attempt() -> tuple[bool, str]:
        run = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(root), "push", "origin", f"HEAD:{branch}"],
                             capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
        return run.returncode == 0, run.stderr.strip()

    ok, err = attempt()
    if ok:
        return True, None
    pull = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(root), "pull", "--no-rebase", "--no-edit",
                           "origin", branch], capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if pull.returncode != 0:
        return False, f"push rejected and the retry merge failed: {pull.stderr.strip() or err}"
    ok, err = attempt()
    if not ok:
        return False, f"push rejected again after merging {branch}: {err}"
    return True, None


def drive_state(root: Path, m: dict, item_id: str) -> tuple[str, str | None, Path]:
    """("no-approval" | "build" | "verify" | "ready", detail, records_dir):
    the next mechanical step for item_id, read from its latest records
    alone. A build only counts as current when it is a pass bound to the
    valid approval's own sha; a verify only counts as current when it is a
    pass bound to that build's own sha -- the same staleness a re-approval
    or a re-build must force, attest's own chain check already enforces
    (check_attest_chain), read here rather than re-typed."""
    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    records_dir = root / records_rel / item_id

    status = approval_status(root, m, item_id)
    if status != "valid":
        return "no-approval", status, records_dir

    latest_approval = latest_record(records_dir, "approval")
    latest_build = latest_record(records_dir, "build")
    build_current = (latest_build is not None and latest_build.get("status") == "pass"
                     and latest_build.get("approval_sha") == (latest_approval or {}).get("sha"))
    if not build_current:
        return "build", None, records_dir

    latest_verify = latest_record(records_dir, "verify")
    verify_current = (latest_verify is not None and latest_verify.get("status") == "pass"
                      and latest_verify.get("build_sha") == latest_build.get("sha"))
    if not verify_current:
        return "verify", None, records_dir

    return "ready", None, records_dir


def _drive_run_stage(root: Path, records_dir: Path, branch: str, stage: str, item_id: str) -> int:
    """Runs build or verify in-process -- the same command `attavo build`
    or `attavo verify` would run, printing the same reason on a refusal,
    failure, or halt. A non-pass record is counseled (cmd_counsel, issue
    #100) and filed (cmd_file_stops, issue #89) before it is transported,
    so a stop's own counsel record and .filed marker -- the filing
    command's stdout, the issue file-stops just opened -- exist on disk
    in time to be committed and pushed in the very same commit as the
    stage record and its log; a marker written only after the push would
    never reach the ledger at all, and a second worktree or a fresh clone
    would refile every stop it finds with no marker beside it. The stage
    record and the counsel record (when one was written) are then
    transported together: committed with commit_record and pushed with
    push_record_commit, whatever the stage record's status -- the record
    pushes stay the same pushes, made by drive in the run that produced
    the record (issue #84's rule 42 row)."""
    before = latest_attempt_path(records_dir, stage)

    # The invocation's marker, committed and pushed before the stage runs:
    # one commit per attempt, so that an invocation which dies or hangs
    # is a fact on the branch (proof five) and not a silence.
    nn = next_attempt_number(records_dir, stage)
    marker = write_invocation_marker(root, records_dir, stage, nn)
    try:
        commit_record(root, [marker], f"attavo: {stage} invocation {nn:02d} started for item {item_id}")
    except RuntimeError as exc:
        print(f"attavo drive: stopped: could not commit the {stage} invocation marker: {exc}")
        return 1
    ok, err = push_record_commit(root, branch)
    if not ok:
        print(f"attavo drive: stopped: could not push the {stage} invocation marker: {err}")
        return 1

    rc = cmd_build([item_id]) if stage == "build" else cmd_verify([item_id])
    after = latest_attempt_path(records_dir, stage)

    if after is None or after == before:
        # Refused before writing anything at all (e.g. no adapter
        # configured) -- the stage command already printed why.
        return rc if rc != 0 else 1

    record = latest_record(records_dir, stage)
    status_out = record.get("status")
    to_commit = [after]
    if status_out != "pass":
        counsel_before = latest_attempt_path(records_dir, "counsel")
        cmd_counsel([item_id])
        counsel_after = latest_attempt_path(records_dir, "counsel")
        if counsel_after is not None and counsel_after != counsel_before:
            to_commit.append(counsel_after)
        # file-stops is its own idempotent read of the item's records, so
        # calling it here on every run that wrote a non-pass record is
        # safe to call again on a later run: an already-filed stop's own
        # marker is what stops it from being filed twice.
        cmd_file_stops([item_id])

    message = f"attavo: {stage} record for item {item_id} ({after.stem})"
    try:
        commit_record(root, to_commit, message)
    except RuntimeError as exc:
        print(f"attavo drive: stopped: could not commit the {stage} record: {exc}")
        return 1

    ok, err = push_record_commit(root, branch)
    if not ok:
        print(f"attavo drive: stopped: could not push the {stage} record: {err}")
        return 1

    if status_out == "halted":
        question = (record.get("halted") or {}).get("question")
        print(f"attavo drive: stopped: {stage} halted: {question} ({after.name}); "
              f"pushed to {branch}")
    elif status_out != "pass":
        reason = record.get("refusal") or status_out
        print(f"attavo drive: stopped: {stage} {status_out}: {reason} ({after.name}); "
              f"pushed to {branch}")
    else:
        print(f"attavo drive: pushed {after.name} to {branch}")
    return rc


def _drive_step(root: Path, m: dict, item_id: str) -> tuple[int, bool]:
    """One mechanical step, exactly as a single `attavo drive` call always
    has -- and whether repetition under --until-ready should stop here:
    True for every refusal or stop (no valid approval, no
    [forge].checks_command, the forge not green, or a stage that wrote a
    non-pass record) and for the ready-to-merge terminal state; False
    only when a stage just passed, so drive_state may have a further
    stage to pursue on the next call."""
    try:
        stage, extra, records_dir = drive_state(root, m, item_id)
    except ShallowCheckoutError as exc:
        print(f"attavo drive: refused: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2, True

    if stage == "no-approval":
        print(f"attavo drive: refused: approval is {extra}")
        return 1, True

    branch = git(root, "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD").strip()

    if stage == "build":
        rc = _drive_run_stage(root, records_dir, branch, "build", item_id)
        return rc, rc != 0

    checks_command = (m.get("forge") or {}).get("checks_command")
    if not checks_command:
        print("attavo drive: refused: no [forge].checks_command in the manifest")
        return 1, True
    head_sha = git(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD").strip()
    forge_checks, ok = poll_forge_checks(root, checks_command, head_sha)
    if not ok:
        conclusions = (", ".join(f"{c['name']} {c['conclusion']}"
                                 for c in forge_checks["conclusions"])
                      or "no checks reported")
        print(f"attavo drive: stopped: forge checks not green on {head_sha[:12]}: {conclusions}")
        return 1, True

    if stage == "verify":
        rc = _drive_run_stage(root, records_dir, branch, "verify", item_id)
        return rc, rc != 0

    print(f"attavo drive: item {item_id} is ready to merge")
    return 0, True


def cmd_drive(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    # Issue #145: Python block-buffers stdout when it is not a tty (a
    # pipe, e.g. a screen reading this process live), so every stage
    # line -- attavo build:, attavo drive: pushed, attavo verify:, attavo
    # drive: item N is ready to merge -- would otherwise sit unflushed
    # until this process exits. Line-buffering makes a pipe reader see
    # each line the moment it is printed, the same as a terminal already
    # does; no other output path changes.
    sys.stdout.reconfigure(line_buffering=True)
    until_ready = "--until-ready" in argv
    recover = "--recover" in argv
    positional = [a for a in argv if a not in ("--until-ready", "--recover")]
    root = Path(".").resolve()
    item_id = positional[0] if positional else derive_item_id(root)
    if not item_id:
        print("attavo drive: cannot derive item id; pass N or run on an attavo/N-* branch",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    m = read_manifest(root)
    if m is None:
        print(f"attavo drive: no {MANIFEST} at {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    if recover:
        return _drive_recover(root, m, item_id)

    # Without --until-ready this is exactly one call to _drive_step,
    # letter for letter what cmd_drive always did: `not until_ready`
    # returns on the very first iteration regardless of `done`. With it,
    # a step that just passed (done False) repeats -- step, wait (the
    # forge poll already inside _drive_step), step, wait -- until the
    # item is ready to merge or a step stops on its own account.
    while True:
        rc, done = _drive_step(root, m, item_id)
        if done or not until_ready:
            return rc


# --- file-findings: one tenant issue per finding, nothing filed silently ---

def cmd_file_findings(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    root = Path(".").resolve()
    item_id = argv[0] if argv else derive_item_id(root)
    if not item_id:
        print("attavo file-findings: cannot derive item id; pass N or run on an attavo/N-* branch",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    m = read_manifest(root)
    if m is None:
        print(f"attavo file-findings: no {MANIFEST} at {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    item_records_dir = root / records_rel / item_id

    forge_cfg = m.get("forge") or {}
    file_issue_command = forge_cfg.get("file_issue_command")
    if not file_issue_command:
        print("attavo file-findings: refused: no [forge].file_issue_command in the manifest")
        return 1

    latest_verify = latest_record(item_records_dir, "verify")
    if latest_verify is None:
        print(f"attavo file-findings: refused: no verify record for item {item_id}")
        return 1

    findings = latest_verify.get("findings") or []
    if not findings:
        print("attavo file-findings: no findings to file")
        return 0

    for finding in findings:
        if not finding.get("retires"):
            title = finding.get("title", "<untitled>")
            print(f"attavo file-findings: refused: finding {title!r} carries no retires; "
                  f"nothing filed")
            return 1

    for finding in findings:
        run = subprocess.run(file_issue_command, shell=True, cwd=str(root),
                             input=json.dumps(finding) + "\n", capture_output=True,
                             text=True, check=False)
        if run.returncode != 0:
            title = finding.get("title", "<untitled>")
            print(f"attavo file-findings: refused: filing {title!r} exited {run.returncode}: "
                  f"{run.stderr.strip()}")
            return 1

    print(f"attavo file-findings: filed {len(findings)} issue(s)")
    return 0


# --- file-stops: one tenant issue per stop record, nothing filed silently,
# nothing filed twice (issue #89) ---

STOP_STAGES = ("build", "verify")
FILE_STOPS_LOG_TAIL_LINES = 20


def compose_stop_title(item_id: str, stage: str, n: int, stop: dict) -> str:
    """`stop <class>/<provenance> on item <N>: <stage>-<NN>` -- the title
    the tenant's file-issue script would otherwise fall back to
    <untitled> for. An empty class or provenance is a record defect, not
    a filing: this returns "" rather than a title with a blank half, so
    the caller can refuse on it."""
    cls = stop.get("class")
    provenance = stop.get("provenance")
    if not cls or not provenance:
        return ""
    return f"stop {cls}/{provenance} on item {item_id}: {stage}-{n:02d}"


def counsel_record_for(item_records_dir: Path, stage: str, n: int) -> dict | None:
    """The latest counsel-NN.json (issue #100) whose own "record" field
    names f"{stage}-{n:02d}" -- the counsel invocation, if any, that read
    this exact stage record -- or None when counsel never ran against it
    (e.g. file-stops run standalone, outside drive)."""
    target = f"{stage}-{n:02d}"
    attempts = parse_attempts(item_records_dir, "counsel")
    matches = [rec for _, rec in sorted(attempts.items()) if rec.get("record") == target]
    return matches[-1] if matches else None


def cmd_file_stops(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    root = Path(".").resolve()
    item_id = argv[0] if argv else derive_item_id(root)
    if not item_id:
        print("attavo file-stops: cannot derive item id; pass N or run on an attavo/N-* branch",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    m = read_manifest(root)
    if m is None:
        print(f"attavo file-stops: no {MANIFEST} at {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    item_records_dir = root / records_rel / item_id

    forge_cfg = m.get("forge") or {}
    file_issue_command = forge_cfg.get("file_issue_command")
    if not file_issue_command:
        print("attavo file-stops: refused: no [forge].file_issue_command in the manifest")
        return 1

    # A stop record's own .filed marker beside it -- the same convention as
    # its .log sidecar -- is what makes a second run file nothing: no new
    # artifact beside the records (the gate output already lives in the
    # log), just a fact about this one record, in the one place a reader
    # already looks. It carries the filing command's own stdout (the
    # issue it opened), so that fact is kept, not just the fact of
    # filing. This command never commits it -- the same as build and
    # verify, which write their own records uncommitted -- a caller that
    # wants it on the ledger commits it itself; drive does, in the same
    # commit as the record and its log (issue #89).
    pending: list[tuple[str, int, dict, Path]] = []
    for stage in STOP_STAGES:
        attempts = parse_attempts(item_records_dir, stage)
        for n in sorted(attempts):
            stop = attempts[n].get("stop")
            if not stop:
                continue
            marker = item_records_dir / f"{stage}-{n:02d}.filed"
            if marker.exists():
                continue
            pending.append((stage, n, stop, marker))

    # A refused spec-gate record is the same shape of stop, earlier (issue
    # #133); its own class is "gate" (issue #137), never persisted -- it
    # never carries a stop field of its own (cmd_spec_gate is untouched
    # by this lane), so it is synthesized here from its refusal.
    for n, rec in sorted(parse_attempts(item_records_dir, "spec").items()):
        if rec.get("status") != "refused":
            continue
        marker = item_records_dir / f"spec-{n:02d}.filed"
        if marker.exists():
            continue
        pending.append(("spec", n, gate_stop(rec.get("refusal")), marker))

    if not pending:
        print("attavo file-stops: no stops to file")
        return 0

    for stage, n, stop, _marker in pending:
        if stop["class"] == "unclassed" and not stop.get("detail"):
            print(f"attavo file-stops: refused: {stage}-{n:02d} is unclassed with no detail; "
                  f"nothing filed")
            return 1

    for stage, n, stop, _marker in pending:
        if not compose_stop_title(item_id, stage, n, stop):
            print(f"attavo file-stops: refused: {stage}-{n:02d} composes an empty title; "
                  f"nothing filed")
            return 1

    for stage, n, stop, marker in pending:
        record_path = item_records_dir / f"{stage}-{n:02d}.json"
        log_path = record_path.with_suffix(".log")
        log_tail = ("\n".join(log_path.read_text().splitlines()[-FILE_STOPS_LOG_TAIL_LINES:])
                   if log_path.is_file() else "")
        counsel = counsel_record_for(item_records_dir, stage, n)
        body = {
            "title": compose_stop_title(item_id, stage, n, stop),
            "class": stop["class"],
            "provenance": stop["provenance"],
            "record": str(record_path.relative_to(root)),
            "log_tail": log_tail,
            "counsel_verdict": counsel.get("verdict") if counsel else None,
            "counsel_citations": counsel.get("citations") if counsel else [],
            "counsel_proposal": counsel.get("proposal") if counsel else None,
        }
        run = subprocess.run(file_issue_command, shell=True, cwd=str(root),
                             input=json.dumps(body) + "\n", capture_output=True,
                             text=True, check=False)
        if run.returncode != 0:
            print(f"attavo file-stops: refused: filing {stage}-{n:02d} exited {run.returncode}: "
                  f"{run.stderr.strip()}")
            return 1
        marker.write_text(run.stdout)

    print(f"attavo file-stops: filed {len(pending)} issue(s)")
    return 0


# --- record-only: whether HEAD, against its first parent, touches only
# paths.records. Core logic about a git checkout, not a forge's shell
# (issue #19); a workflow template's push-and-retrigger guard is one line
# calling this instead of carrying its own copy. ---

def cmd_record_only(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    root = Path(".").resolve()
    m = read_manifest(root)
    if m is None:
        print(f"attavo record-only: no {MANIFEST} at {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    try:
        changed = git(root, "diff", "--name-only", "HEAD^1", "HEAD").splitlines()
    except RuntimeError as exc:
        print(f"attavo record-only: refused: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    prefix = records_rel.rstrip("/") + "/"
    only_records = all(p == records_rel or p.startswith(prefix) for p in changed if p)
    print("attavo record-only: yes" if only_records else "attavo record-only: no")
    return 0 if only_records else 1


# --- attest: the signed envelope over one item's whole traversal ---

def derive_attest_items(root: Path, records_rel: str) -> tuple[set[str], str | None]:
    """The item ids from the merge commit's own first-parent diff -- record
    paths under records_rel/<N>/, not a branch name, which a squash merge
    discards and which names a forge's convention the product does not
    know. Returns (items, None), else (empty set, reason) only when the
    diff itself could not be read -- zero items found is not an error
    here (issue #58): not every merge is an item merge, and the caller
    decides what zero, one, or more than one means."""
    try:
        changed = git(root, "diff", "--name-only", "HEAD^1", "HEAD").splitlines()
    except RuntimeError as exc:
        return set(), f"git: {exc}"
    prefix = records_rel.rstrip("/") + "/"
    items: set[str] = set()
    for p in changed:
        if p.startswith(prefix):
            rest = p[len(prefix):]
            item = rest.split("/", 1)[0]
            if item:
                items.add(item)
    return items, None


NOT_YET = object()


def check_attest_chain(records_dir: Path, item_id: str, partial: bool = False):
    """The two assertions attavo attest refuses on: the latest spec,
    approval, build, and verify records for item_id all exist, and
    build's approval_sha names the latest approval -- a re-approval after
    a build must force a re-run of what changed. Returns (None, message)
    on either of them, the message being the same text attest prints, not
    a copy of it a caller re-types (issue #74: proof two reuses it);
    returns ((spec, approval, build, verify), None) when the chain holds.

    A verify record can only name a build that existed when it was
    written (issue #109), so a latest verify whose build_sha is not the
    latest build's sha is never a corrupt chain -- it is a verify
    superseded by a later build, and is treated as absent: the next
    stage is verify, not a refusal, exactly as if no verify record
    existed yet.

    partial (issue #67) is for a pull request, where a later stage's
    record cannot exist yet because the stage that would produce it is
    itself waiting on this check. Under partial, a missing (or
    superseded) record is not a refusal -- the chain assertion still
    runs over whatever records exist, and (NOT_YET, message) is
    returned, the message naming the item and the stages present and
    absent -- or, for a superseded verify, the superseded record and the
    build it named -- when a stage is still missing once that assertion
    holds."""
    latest_spec = latest_record(records_dir, "spec")
    latest_approval = latest_record(records_dir, "approval")
    latest_build = latest_record(records_dir, "build")
    latest_verify = latest_record(records_dir, "verify")

    # A verify naming a sha other than the latest build's is superseded:
    # treated as absent for the chain, never as a mismatch.
    superseded = None
    if latest_build is not None and latest_verify is not None:
        named_sha = latest_verify.get("build_sha")
        if named_sha != latest_build.get("sha"):
            verify_n = max(parse_attempts(records_dir, "verify"))
            build_attempts = parse_attempts(records_dir, "build")
            named_build_n = next((n for n, rec in build_attempts.items()
                                  if rec.get("sha") == named_sha), None)
            named_build = f"build-{named_build_n:02d}" if named_build_n is not None else named_sha
            superseded = (verify_n, named_sha, named_build, max(build_attempts))
            latest_verify = None

    stages = (("spec", latest_spec), ("approval", latest_approval),
              ("build", latest_build), ("verify", latest_verify))

    if not partial:
        for stage, rec in stages:
            if rec is None:
                if stage == "verify" and superseded is not None:
                    verify_n, named_sha, _, _ = superseded
                    return None, (f"no verify names the latest build "
                                 f"(verify-{verify_n:02d} names {named_sha})")
                return None, f"no {stage} record for item {item_id}"

    if latest_approval is not None and latest_build is not None:
        if latest_build.get("approval_sha") != latest_approval.get("sha"):
            return None, ("build's approval_sha does not name the latest approval; a "
                          "re-approval after the build must force a re-run")

    missing = [stage for stage, rec in stages if rec is None]
    if partial and missing:
        present = [stage for stage, rec in stages if rec is not None]
        if missing == ["verify"] and superseded is not None:
            verify_n, _, named_build, latest_build_n = superseded
            return NOT_YET, (f"item {item_id} not yet (present: {', '.join(present) or 'none'}; "
                             f"verify-{verify_n:02d} names {named_build}, "
                             f"superseded by build-{latest_build_n:02d})")
        return NOT_YET, (f"item {item_id} not yet (present: {', '.join(present) or 'none'}; "
                         f"absent: {', '.join(missing)})")

    return (latest_spec, latest_approval, latest_build, latest_verify), None


def cmd_attest(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    partial = "--partial" in argv
    positional = [a for a in argv if not a.startswith("--")]
    unknown = [a for a in argv if a.startswith("--") and a != "--partial"]
    if unknown:
        print(f"attavo attest: unknown argument {unknown[0]!r}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    root = Path(".").resolve()
    m = read_manifest(root)
    if m is None:
        print(f"attavo attest: no {MANIFEST} at {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    item_id = positional[0] if positional else None
    if item_id is None:
        items, err = derive_attest_items(root, records_rel)
        if err:
            print(f"attavo attest: refused: {err}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        # Not every merge is an item merge -- a merge that carries no item
        # at all is an ordinary fact, not a defect (issue #58); the
        # attest template runs this on every push and has nothing to
        # sign or commit when nothing was attested, and its existing
        # `if:` guards already read this step's own exit code.
        if not items:
            print("attavo attest: no item derived from the merge commit's record paths; "
                  "nothing to attest")
            return 0
        if len(items) > 1:
            print(f"attavo attest: refused: {len(items)} items derived from the merge "
                  f"commit's record paths: {', '.join(sorted(items))}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        item_id = next(iter(items))

    records_dir = root / records_rel / item_id
    chain, message = check_attest_chain(records_dir, item_id, partial=partial)
    if chain is None:
        print(f"attavo attest: refused: {message}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if chain is NOT_YET:
        print(f"attavo attest --partial: {message}")
        return 0
    latest_spec, latest_approval, latest_build, latest_verify = chain

    spec_path = latest_attempt_path(records_dir, "spec")
    approval_path = latest_attempt_path(records_dir, "approval")
    build_path = latest_attempt_path(records_dir, "build")
    verify_path = latest_attempt_path(records_dir, "verify")

    # sha and merge_sha are the same commit here -- the one this statement
    # was computed at. On the merge group that commit is the one main will
    # hold; kept as two fields because a forge-specific template signs
    # this statement against "the commit that lands", naming it merge_sha
    # is what makes that unambiguous to a reader who has never seen attest
    # run anywhere but the merge group.
    head_sha = git(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD").strip()

    statement = {
        "sha": head_sha,
        "merge_sha": head_sha,
        "item": item_id,
        "spec": spec_path.stem if spec_path else None,
        "spec_sha": latest_spec.get("sha"),
        "approval": approval_path.stem if approval_path else None,
        "approval_sha": latest_approval.get("sha"),
        "build": build_path.stem if build_path else None,
        "build_sha": latest_build.get("sha"),
        "verify": verify_path.stem if verify_path else None,
        "verify_sha": latest_verify.get("sha"),
        "at": now_iso(),
        # Neither is populated by anything today -- no ruling or amendment
        # log exists yet for either to read from. Carried as empty rather
        # than omitted, since the schema names them.
        "interventions": [],
        "acts": [],
        "tokens_by_stage": {"build": latest_build.get("tokens"), "verify": latest_verify.get("tokens")},
        # Never summed across stages -- the 2026-09-09 ruling on reporting
        # across vendors: a stage's executor may report no cost at all.
        "cost_by_stage_usd": {"build": latest_build.get("cost_usd"), "verify": latest_verify.get("cost_usd")},
        "wall_clock_by_stage_s": {"build": latest_build.get("wall_clock_s"),
                                  "verify": latest_verify.get("wall_clock_s")},
        "product_version": product_version(),
        "product_pin": (m.get("attavo") or {}).get("version"),
    }
    print(json.dumps(statement, indent=2))
    return 0


# --- ledger: every act on an item by identity and class (issue #128) ---

# operator acts (a person's own decisions and transport) vs. product acts
# (the driver's or the records app's own automated commits) vs. the
# executor's own code changes -- the three totals the plan's status table
# and every reviewing session used to count by hand. record-or-launch
# (below) contributes to neither: an honest count never guesses which
# side of that line a pre-v0.18.0 commit falls on.
LEDGER_OPERATOR_CLASSES = ("approve", "amend", "launch", "transport", "merge")
LEDGER_V018_CUTOFF = (0, 18, 0)


def _semver_tuple(version: str | None) -> tuple[int, int, int] | None:
    m = re.match(r"^v(\d+)\.(\d+)\.(\d+)", version or "")
    return (int(m.group(1)), int(m.group(2)), int(m.group(3))) if m else None


def _is_records_app(name: str) -> bool:
    """The forge's own app-bot login -- exactly the identity the
    attavo-spec-gate and attavo-approve templates commit spec-gate and
    approval records as (an app-token step mints it at run time from the
    tenant's own app credentials): "<app-slug>[bot]". Never a name this
    product can name in advance, so the one fact it can check is the
    shape the forge itself gives every app identity, not a specific one."""
    return name.endswith("[bot]")


def first_commit_touching(root: Path, relpath: str) -> str | None:
    """The earliest commit touching relpath -- the mirror of
    last_commit_touching, which answers the latest. Ledger's own walk
    starts here: the item's first spec commit."""
    out = git(root, "log", "--reverse", "--format=%H", "--", relpath)
    lines = out.split()
    return lines[0] if lines else None


def _record_blobs_at(root: Path, specs: list[tuple[str, str]], item_id: str) -> dict[tuple[str, str], dict]:
    """{(sha, relpath): record dict} for every (sha, relpath) pair, read by
    one `git cat-file --batch` process for the whole list rather than one
    `git show` per record (issue #153, ledger's only need for a record at a
    past commit rather than the working tree -- issue #128). Each blob is
    still spilled to a throwaway file and read through _load_record (issue
    #60, acceptance 6: the one place a record's bytes become a dict),
    rather than a second json.loads of its own. A pair git cannot resolve,
    or whose blob is not valid JSON, is simply absent from the result --
    the same silence _record_at once gave a missing blob."""
    if not specs:
        return {}
    stdin = "".join(f"{sha}:{relpath}\n" for sha, relpath in specs)
    out = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(root), "cat-file", "--batch"],
                         input=stdin.encode(), capture_output=True, check=False)
    data = out.stdout
    pos = 0
    result: dict[tuple[str, str], dict] = {}
    for key in specs:
        newline = data.index(b"\n", pos)
        header = data[pos:newline].decode()
        pos = newline + 1
        parts = header.split(" ")
        if parts[-1] == "missing":
            continue
        size = int(parts[-1])
        content = data[pos:pos + size]
        pos += size + 1
        with tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile("wb", suffix=".json", delete=False) as fh:
            fh.write(content)
            blob_path = Path(fh.name)
        try:
            result[key] = _load_record(blob_path, item_id)
        except json.JSONDecodeError:
            continue
        finally:
            blob_path.unlink(missing_ok=True)
    return result


def item_ledger(root: Path, m: dict, item_id: str, merges: list[dict] | None = None) -> dict:
    """{"item", "rows", "summary"}: one row per act between item_id's first
    spec commit and its merge (merge_introducing_records, the same
    derivation doctor's proof two and attest both already read) or HEAD
    when the item has not merged yet.

    merges, when given, is _merges_reachable's own result -- cmd_ledger
    computes it once and passes it here for every item, rather than each
    item walking HEAD's merges again (issue #153).

    Each commit gets exactly one class, in this order: the merge commit
    itself is "merge"; a commit authored by the executor identity
    (executor_identity) is "change"; a commit introducing an approval
    record is "approve", whoever committed it -- an operator's decision,
    however it reached git; a commit introducing a spec-gate, build, or
    verify record, authored by the driver identity (driver_identity) or
    the records app, is "record"; the same, authored by a person, is
    "launch" for build or verify (spec-gate records the app always
    commits, in every template this product ships) -- unless that
    record's own product_version predates v0.18.0, when driver_identity
    did not yet exist to tell the two apart, and the row is
    "record-or-launch" with a note, counted in neither summary total
    rather than guessed; a person's commit touching the spec is "spec"
    before the first approval record and "amend" after; anything else by
    a person is "transport" (a merge of main among them)."""
    specs_rel = m["paths"]["specs"]
    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    specs_prefix = f"{specs_rel.rstrip('/')}/{item_id}/"
    records_prefix = f"{records_rel.rstrip('/')}/{item_id}/"
    empty_summary = {"operator_acts": 0, "product_acts": 0, "executor_commits": 0,
                     "stops": 0, "since": None}

    first_spec_sha = first_commit_touching(root, str(Path(specs_rel) / item_id))
    if first_spec_sha is None:
        return {"item": item_id, "rows": [], "summary": empty_summary}

    if merges is None:
        merges, refusal = _merges_reachable(root)
        if refusal:
            return {"item": item_id, "rows": [], "summary": empty_summary}
    merge_sha, _ = merge_introducing_records(root, records_rel, item_id, STAGES, merges=merges)
    if merge_sha is not None:
        branch_tip = next(mrg["parents"][1] for mrg in merges if mrg["sha"] == merge_sha)
        commits = commits_with_diffs(root, f"{first_spec_sha}^", end=branch_tip)
        commits += commits_with_diffs(root, f"{merge_sha}^1", end=merge_sha)
    else:
        commits = commits_with_diffs(root, f"{first_spec_sha}^", end="HEAD")

    driver = driver_identity()

    # Every record a walked commit touches, read by one batched process
    # for the whole item rather than one `git show` per record (issue
    # #153): a first pass over the already-fetched diffs finds which
    # commits touch a record, then _record_blobs_at fetches them all.
    record_touches: dict[str, tuple[str, str]] = {}
    for c in commits:
        if merge_sha is not None and c["sha"] == merge_sha:
            continue
        if c["email"].startswith("executor+") and c["email"].endswith("@attavo.invalid"):
            continue
        for p in c["touched"]:
            if not p.startswith(records_prefix):
                continue
            rm = RECORD_RE.match(Path(p).name)
            if rm and rm.group(1) in STAGES:
                record_touches[c["sha"]] = (rm.group(1), p)
                break
    record_content = _record_blobs_at(
        root, [(sha, relpath) for sha, (_, relpath) in record_touches.items()], item_id)

    rows: list[dict] = []
    operator_acts = product_acts = executor_commits = 0
    since = None
    approval_seen = False

    for c in commits:
        sha, name, email = c["sha"], c["name"], c["email"]

        if merge_sha is not None and sha == merge_sha:
            rows.append({"sha": sha, "identity": name, "class": "merge"})
            operator_acts += 1
            continue

        if email.startswith("executor+") and email.endswith("@attavo.invalid"):
            rows.append({"sha": sha, "identity": name, "class": "change"})
            executor_commits += 1
            continue

        record_touch = record_touches.get(sha)

        if record_touch is not None:
            stage, relpath = record_touch
            content = record_content.get((sha, relpath), {})
            if since is None:
                since = content.get("product_version")

            if stage == "approval":
                rows.append({"sha": sha, "identity": name, "class": "approve"})
                operator_acts += 1
                approval_seen = True
                continue

            is_driver = name == driver["name"] and email == driver["email"]
            is_records_app = _is_records_app(name)
            if stage == "spec" or is_driver or is_records_app:
                rows.append({"sha": sha, "identity": name, "class": "record"})
                product_acts += 1
                continue

            version = _semver_tuple(content.get("product_version"))
            if version is not None and version < LEDGER_V018_CUTOFF:
                rows.append({"sha": sha, "identity": name, "class": "record-or-launch",
                            "note": "not distinguishable before v0.18.0"})
                continue

            rows.append({"sha": sha, "identity": name, "class": "launch"})
            operator_acts += 1
            continue

        # An invocation marker's own commit (proof five): the product's
        # act, never the operator's.
        if any(p.startswith(records_prefix) and p.endswith(INVOCATION_MARKER_SUFFIX)
               for p in c["touched"]):
            rows.append({"sha": sha, "identity": name, "class": "record"})
            product_acts += 1
            continue

        if any(p.startswith(specs_prefix) for p in c["touched"]):
            cls = "amend" if approval_seen else "spec"
            rows.append({"sha": sha, "identity": name, "class": cls})
            if cls == "amend":
                operator_acts += 1
            continue

        rows.append({"sha": sha, "identity": name, "class": "transport"})
        operator_acts += 1

    # issue #137: the same set attavo stops counts, from the same reader
    # (item_stops) -- not a second, independent count kept in sync by
    # hand. One non-pass record per row, whatever its stage; a gate
    # refusal is included the same as a non-pass build or verify record.
    stops = len(item_stops(root, m, item_id)["rows"])

    summary = {"operator_acts": operator_acts, "product_acts": product_acts,
              "executor_commits": executor_commits, "stops": stops, "since": since}
    return {"item": item_id, "rows": rows, "summary": summary}


def cmd_ledger(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    as_json = "--json" in argv
    positional = [a for a in argv if a != "--json"]
    root = Path(".").resolve()
    m = read_manifest(root)
    if m is None:
        print(f"attavo ledger: no {MANIFEST} at {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    if positional:
        item_ids = [positional[0]]
    else:
        records_root = root / records_rel
        item_ids = (sorted((p.name for p in records_root.iterdir() if p.is_dir()))
                   if records_root.is_dir() else [])

    # Computed once for every item in this read, not once per item (issue
    # #153): the default branch's merges do not depend on which item is
    # being asked about.
    merges, refusal = _merges_reachable(root)
    if refusal:
        print(f"attavo ledger: refused: {refusal}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    ledgers = [item_ledger(root, m, item_id, merges=merges) for item_id in item_ids]

    if as_json:
        if positional:
            print(json.dumps(ledgers[0], indent=2))
        else:
            print(json.dumps({"items": ledgers}, indent=2))
        return 0

    for entry in ledgers:
        print(f"attavo ledger: item {entry['item']}")
        for row in entry["rows"]:
            note = f"  ({row['note']})" if "note" in row else ""
            print(f"  {row['sha'][:12]}  {row['identity']:<32}  {row['class']}{note}")
        s = entry["summary"]
        print(f"  operator acts: {s['operator_acts']}  product acts: {s['product_acts']}  "
             f"executor commits: {s['executor_commits']}  stops: {s['stops']}  "
             f"since: {s['since']}")
    if not ledgers:
        print(f"attavo ledger: no item found under {records_rel}")
    return 0


# --- stops: every non-pass record by class, provenance, and answerer
# (issue #129) ---

def item_stops(root: Path, m: dict, item_id: str) -> dict:
    """{"item", "rows", "totals"}: one row per non-pass build or verify
    record (STOP_STAGES), plus every refused spec-gate record (issue
    #133), class "gate" (issue #137) -- read from status and refusal
    rather than a stop field spec-gate records never carry (gate_stop
    synthesizes it) -- under item_id, whatever the record's own
    record_version -- a record too old to carry stop at all still gets
    a row, its class printed as the doctor's own phrase for it.

    The answerer, a closed choice: "counsel:<verdict>" when
    counsel_record_for finds a counsel record naming this exact stage and
    number -- the product's own answer, which wins over whatever the stop
    itself says, since a counseled stop was already read once; failing
    that, "person" for class question (a person's to answer, not a
    guard's); failing that, "guard" for a class in GUARDED_STOP_CLASSES
    (a guard's own rejection needed no further reading); anything else
    -- no-cause, unclassed, or no stop at all -- is "none": nobody has
    answered it yet."""
    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    item_records_dir = root / records_rel / item_id
    by_class = {c: 0 for c in stop_class_enum()}
    by_answerer: dict[str, int] = {}
    before_stop_was_recorded = 0
    rows: list[dict] = []

    def add_row(stage: str, n: int, stop: dict | None) -> None:
        nonlocal before_stop_was_recorded
        name = f"{stage}-{n:02d}"
        marker = item_records_dir / f"{name}.filed"
        filed_issue = marker.read_text() if marker.is_file() else None
        counsel = counsel_record_for(item_records_dir, stage, n)

        if counsel is not None:
            answerer = f"counsel:{counsel.get('verdict')}"
        elif stop and stop.get("class") == "question":
            answerer = "person"
        elif stop and stop.get("class") in GUARDED_STOP_CLASSES:
            answerer = "guard"
        else:
            answerer = "none"

        if stop:
            cls, provenance, detail = (stop.get("class"), stop.get("provenance"),
                                      stop.get("detail"))
            if cls in by_class:
                by_class[cls] += 1
        else:
            cls, provenance, detail = "before stop was recorded", None, None
            before_stop_was_recorded += 1

        by_answerer[answerer] = by_answerer.get(answerer, 0) + 1
        rows.append({"item": item_id, "record": name, "class": cls,
                    "provenance": provenance, "detail": detail,
                    "answerer": answerer, "filed_issue": filed_issue})

    if item_records_dir.is_dir():
        for stage in STOP_STAGES:
            for n, rec in sorted(parse_attempts(item_records_dir, stage).items()):
                if rec.get("status") == "pass":
                    continue
                add_row(stage, n, rec.get("stop"))
        for n, rec in sorted(parse_attempts(item_records_dir, "spec").items()):
            if rec.get("status") != "refused":
                continue
            add_row("spec", n, gate_stop(rec.get("refusal")))

    totals = {"by_class": by_class, "by_answerer": by_answerer,
             "before_stop_was_recorded": before_stop_was_recorded}
    return {"item": item_id, "rows": rows, "totals": totals}


def cmd_stops(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    as_json = "--json" in argv
    positional = [a for a in argv if a != "--json"]
    root = Path(".").resolve()
    m = read_manifest(root)
    if m is None:
        print(f"attavo stops: no {MANIFEST} at {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    records_rel = m["paths"]["records"]
    if positional:
        item_ids = [positional[0]]
    else:
        records_root = root / records_rel
        item_ids = (sorted((p.name for p in records_root.iterdir() if p.is_dir()))
                   if records_root.is_dir() else [])

    reports = [item_stops(root, m, item_id) for item_id in item_ids]

    if as_json:
        if positional:
            print(json.dumps(reports[0], indent=2))
        else:
            print(json.dumps({"items": reports}, indent=2))
        return 0

    for entry in reports:
        print(f"attavo stops: item {entry['item']}")
        for row in entry["rows"]:
            filed = f"  filed: {row['filed_issue'].strip()}" if row["filed_issue"] else ""
            print(f"  {row['record']:<10} {row['class']:<24} {row['provenance'] or '-':<10} "
                 f"{row['answerer']:<16}{filed}")
        t = entry["totals"]
        print(f"  by class: {t['by_class']}")
        print(f"  by answerer: {t['by_answerer']}")
        print(f"  before stop was recorded: {t['before_stop_was_recorded']}")
    if not reports:
        print(f"attavo stops: no item found under {records_rel}")
    return 0


def main(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    if not argv or argv[0] in ("-h", "--help"):
        print(__doc__.strip())
        return 0 if argv else 2
    if argv[0] == "doctor":
        return cmd_doctor(argv[1:])
    if argv[0] == "spec-gate":
        return cmd_spec_gate(argv[1:])
    if argv[0] == "approve":
        return cmd_approve(argv[1:])
    if argv[0] == "approval-status":
        return cmd_approval_status(argv[1:])
    if argv[0] == "build":
        return cmd_build(argv[1:])
    if argv[0] == "verify":
        return cmd_verify(argv[1:])
    if argv[0] == "file-findings":
        return cmd_file_findings(argv[1:])
    if argv[0] == "file-stops":
        return cmd_file_stops(argv[1:])
    if argv[0] == "counsel":
        return cmd_counsel(argv[1:])
    if argv[0] == "record-only":
        return cmd_record_only(argv[1:])
    if argv[0] == "attest":
        return cmd_attest(argv[1:])
    if argv[0] == "drive":
        return cmd_drive(argv[1:])
    if argv[0] == "ledger":
        return cmd_ledger(argv[1:])
    if argv[0] == "stops":
        return cmd_stops(argv[1:])
    print(f"attavo: unknown subcommand {argv[0]!r}; only doctor, spec-gate, approve, "
          f"approval-status, build, verify, file-findings, file-stops, counsel, record-only, "
          f"attest, drive, ledger, stops exist at this commit", file=sys.stderr)
    return 2


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
